"Passage 1:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThere is an important statement.\" [3092] \"What?\" I asked. \"Who said it? When?\" [3093] \"At the appropriate time, dear.\" [3094] \"Please tell me, Monsieur Poirot,\" the inspector politely returned to the topic. [3095] \"If you draw a circle around number 19, all the people inside it could have killed Mr. Corry. Mrs. Hemm, the Brandt couple, the MacNodon couple, Miss Watrous. But most importantly, some people have already been set up in the scene. Miss Pemerton may have killed him before going out at about 1:35 p.m., and Miss Webb may have been arranged to meet him, killing him before running out of the room to call the police.\" [3096] \"Ah,\" said the detective, \"now you're getting to the specifics.\" [3097] \"Of course,\" said Poirot, sliding his wheelchair, \"you, my dear Colwyn. You were also on the scene. Finding the lower half of the house on the upper half of the street.\" [3098] \"Oh, really?\" I said indignantly, \"What do you mean?\" [3099] \"I mean that I know all there is to know!\" Poirot declared pompously. [3100] \"And I'm the one who's still trying to tell you the whole thing!\" [3101] \"Murderers are usually arrogant,\" Poirot pointed out, \"and may well play games with you so you have a reason to laugh at me.\" [3102] \"If you go on like this, you'll convince me,\" I said. [3103] I began to feel a little uneasy. [3104] Poirot turned to Inspector Hercule. [3105] \"I say to myself, essentially, it's a simple case of murder. The appearance of the clock, the time advanced by an hour, the deliberate discovery of the body, all of this must be put aside. The essential thing is that an ordinary middle-aged man has died and that someone wanted him to die. If we knew who the dead man was, it would suggest who the murderer is. If he was a notorious con artist, we would have to find those he may have cheated; if he was a detective, we would look for those who had committed a crime under his nose; if he was a rich man, we would look among his heirs. But if we don't know who the man was, finding a suspect from this vast pool is a very daunting task.\" [3106] \"Without considering Miss Pemukh and Miss Hila Webber, who should not have been there, the answer is disappointing. I think only Mr. Lymes has something strange?\" Poirot looked at me inquiringly, and I nodded. \"Everyone has a reason to be trusted. Brand is a famous local architect, MacNorton is a professor at Cambridge University, Mrs. Hamm is the widow of a local auctioneer, and the Waterhouse siblings are respected locals. So let's go back to Mr. Curry. Where does he come from? What brought him to 19 Wilbraham Crescent? The neighbor, Mrs. Hamm, said something very revealing when she found out that the dead man was not living at number 19. 'Oh! I see. He came here to die. How strange.' She has a talent for seeing the essence of things, a talent that only those who are only concerned with their own actions and do not care about others possess. She summed up the whole case. Mr. Curry came to 19 Wilbraham Crescent to die. That's all there is to it!\" [3107] \"Her words suddenly startled me,\" I said. [3108] Poirot did not notice me. [3109] \" 'Dear, dear, dear, come to die.' Mr. Curry came and was killed. But that's not all. His identity cannot be confirmed, which is important. He had no wallet, no identification, and the brand name on his clothes had been torn off. But that's not enough. The card of an insurance agent named Curry was just a temporary measure. If the man's identity cannot be confirmed, he will eventually be given a false identity. It could be his brother, his sister, or his wife. As for the wife, Mrs. Rivale, just the name may have already aroused suspicion. There is a village called Curry-Rivale in Somerset, where I and a friend live nearby \u2013 I don't know what the two names suggest, Mr. Curry, Mrs. Rivale.\" [3110] \"At this point, the plan is almost clear, but what confuses me is why our killer assumes that the police will never be able to determine the actual identity of the deceased. If the man had no family, at least there would be a butler, a servant, or a business partner. This leads me to another assumption: no one knows that the man has disappeared. Another assumption is that he is not British, but only a traveler in this country. This fits with the fact that he had traces of having his teeth treated, but there was no record of any treatment.\" [3111] \"My mind began to form a vague picture of the victim and the killer. The case was carefully planned and skillfully executed, but now there is a flaw that the killer did not anticipate.\" [3112] \"What?\" Hercule asked. [3113] To my surprise, at this point Poirot tilted his head back dramatically and recited a poem:\n[3114] A nail lost, a shoe lost,\n[3115] A shoe lost, a horse lost,\n[3116] One war horse was lost, one war was lost,\n[3117] One war was lost, one empire was lost,\n[3118] All because one horseshoe nail was missing. [3119] He leaned forward. [3120] \"There may be many suspects for the murder of Mr. Curry. But there is only one who could have killed, or had a reason to kill, the girl Iona.\" [3121] We all turned to him at the same time. [3122] \"Let's consider Cavenish Typewriting Agency. There are eight girls working there. On September 9th, four of them were out on an errand, so they wouldn't be back until after lunch. Normally, they would have lunch between 12:30 and 1:30 p.m. The other four, Hilary Webb, Ingrid Brent, and two others, would have lunch between 1:30 and 2:30 p.m. But on that day, Ingrid Brent had a small accident on her way back to the office. Her shoe heel got stuck in the grating, making it difficult for her to walk. So she bought some bread and went straight back to the office.\" [3123] Poirot wagged his strong finger at us. [3124] \"We know that Ingrid Brent was worried about something. She tried to meet Hilary Webb outside the office, but failed. Could this be related to Hilary Webb? We couldn't find any evidence. She might have just wanted to ask Hilary Webb about something that was bothering her. But there was one thing that was clear. She wanted to talk to Hilary Webb outside the office.\" [3125] \"Her statement to the police, that morning, was the only clue we had to what was bothering her. She said, 'I don't know if what she said is true.' Three women testified that day. Ingrid might have been referring to Miss Pemshaw. Or, as we had previously surmised, she might have been referring to Hilary Webb. But there was a third possibility. She might have been referring to Miss Martin.\" [3126] \"Miss Martin? But her testimony only lasted a few minutes.\" [3127] \"Indeed, she only stated the phone call she received claiming it was from Miss Pemerton.\" [3128] \"Are you saying that Ina knew that the phone call was not from Miss Pemerton?\" [3129] \"My inference is even simpler. I believe there was no such phone call at all.\" [3130] He continued speaking:\n[3131] \"Ina's heel broke off. The grille was close to the office. So she returned to the typing pool quickly. But Miss Martindeer was in her own office, unaware that Ina had returned. As far as she was concerned, there was only herself in the office at that time. All she had to do was say that a call came in at 1:49. Ina did not know the severity of the situation at first. Hill went to see Miss Martindeer, who was told there was an appointment to go out. The nature and time of the appointment, Ina did not know. [3132] \"Then the news of the murder broke, and the story unfolded little by little. Miss Pemarho called and asked Hill to come to her. But Miss Pemarho said she had not made the call. The call was said to have come at 2:50 p.m. But Ina knew it was not true. There was no call at that time. Miss Martindeer must have been mistaken, but Miss Martindeer was sure she was not mistaken. The more Ina thought about it, the more puzzled she became. She had to ask Hill. Hill might know. [3133] \"Then the interrogation began. All the girls attended. Miss Martindeer repeated her statement about the call, but Ina knew that the clear evidence and precise time she provided were false. So she went to ask the officer to speak to the chief inspector. I think it's likely that Miss Martindeer overheard her question as she left with the crowd. Or perhaps she heard the girls talking about Ina breaking her heel at that moment, realizing the truth. Anyway, she followed the girl to Wilbraham Crescent. I wonder why Ina went there? [3134] \"Just to see the scene of the murder, I guess,\" Hakaste sighed. [3135] \"Yes, that's right. Maybe Miss Martindeer was talking to her, and they were walking down the street together when Ina casually expressed her doubts. Miss Martindeer decided to take immediate action. They happened to be walking past a phone booth. She said, 'This is very important. You must call the police immediately. Tell them we are going to see them right now.' Following someone's instructions was a habit Ina had formed. She went inside, picked up the phone, and Miss Martindeer followed her, standing behind her, tightening her scarf around her neck, and choking her to death.\" [3136] \"Did nobody see?\" [3137] Poirot shrugged. [3138] \"It should have been someone who saw! But it was exactly 1:00. Lunchtime. And people on New Moon Street were busy looking at this and that. This gave the bold and shameless person the opportunity to act.\" [3139] Hercule Poirot shook his head, incredulous. [3140] \"Miss Martin? I don't understand how she got involved in this case?\" [3141] \"Indeed, it was not immediately apparent. But there is no doubt that Miss Marple killed Ingrid. Oh, yes. Only she could have killed Ingrid, so she must be involved. From Miss Marple, I began to suspect that this murder was a Macbethian[1] one, a cruel and unimaginative boring woman.\u201d\n[3142] \"Lacking imagination?\" Hercule Poirot asked. [3143] \"Oh, yes, very lacking. But very efficient. A first-rate conspirator.\u201d\n[3144] \"But the motive? What was her reason for doing it?\" [3145] Hercule Poirot looks at me. He wags a finger. [3146] \"So, your neighbors' gossip was of no use to you, was it? I found a statement that intrigued me. Do you remember when talking about living abroad, Mrs. Brand said she liked living in Crofton because she had an aunt there. But Mrs. Brand actually had no aunt. She inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada, who was her only surviving relative in the family.\" [3147] Hercule Poirot sat up alertly. [3148] \"So you think \u2013\"\n[3149] Poirot leaned back in his chair, folding his fingers together. He half-closed his eyes, as if dreaming. [3150] \"For example, you are a man, a very ordinary, cautious man, with financial difficulties. One day, you receive a letter from a law firm saying that your wife has inherited a large sum of money from her uncle in Canada. The letter was addressed to Mrs. Brand, but the problem is that the Mrs. Brand who received the letter was not the one mentioned in the letter. She was the second wife, not the first. Imagine how sad and frustrating that would be! But suddenly, there's a solution. Who would know that this was not Mrs. Brand? In Crofton, no one knew that Mr. Brand had been married before. His first marriage was many years ago, during the war, when he was overseas. His wife died soon after they married, and he remarried quickly. He had the original marriage certificate, various other documents, and photos of all the relatives in Canada. Everything was in order. Anyway, it was worth taking a risk. They tried, and succeeded. They went through all the legal procedures. The Brand couple became rich, and their financial difficulties ended \u2013\"\n[3151] \"However, a year later, something unexpected happened to them. What happened? I assume that someone from Canada came to this country and knew all the details about the first Mrs. Brand, and the truth was about to come out. He could be an elderly family executor or a close friend of the family, but whoever he is, he would know. Perhaps they thought they could avoid meeting. Mrs. Brand could pretend to be sick, or she could go abroad, but any similar thing would arouse suspicion. The visitor would insist on seeing the woman he had come to visit specially.\" [3152] \"So, they decided to murder him?\" [3153] \"Yes. Here, I think Mrs. Brand's sister is the mastermind behind the plot.\" [3154] \"You mean Miss Martin and Mrs. Brand are sisters?\" [3155] \"Only then does the story make sense.\" [3156] \"When I saw Mrs. Brand, she reminded me of someone,\" said Hercule Poirot, \"although their personalities were very different, but upon careful reflection, they were somewhat alike. But how could they hope to escape? Someone is missing. They would face interrogation \u2013\"\n[3157] \"If this person went abroad, perhaps just for a vacation, not for business, his schedule would not be fixed. Receiving letters from one place, then postcards from another. By the time people start to suspect, a long time may have passed. Who would associate the already-identified Harry Castleton with the wealthy tourist from Canada who never even set foot in the country? If I were the killer, I would go to France or Belgium for a carefree day of sightseeing, and then deliberately throw the dead man's passport in a train or a car, so that the interrogation could take place there.\" [3158] I unconsciously moved, and Poirot stared at me intently. [3159] \"Right?\" he said. [3160] \"Brand told me he had gone to Bournemouth for a day trip with a blonde woman, and I thought \u2013\"\n[3161] \"That's perfectly normal. Don't doubt it, it's his old habit.\" [3162] \"But these are just conjectures,\" Hercule countered. [3163] \"But you can do your own investigation,\" Hercule said. [3164] He picked up a hotel stationery sheet from the shelf in front of him and handed it to Hercule. [3165] \"You can write to Mr. Endebie at 10 Innisfree Gardens, SW7, who has promised to investigate for me in Canada. He is a well-known international lawyer.\" [3166] \"What about those clocks then?\" [3167] \"Oh! Those clocks! Those remarkable clocks!\" Poirot laughed. \"I think you will find that Miss Rosemary will be held responsible for them. Hence this case, as I said, is quite simple, only made to look fantastic. That clock that Miss Hyde took to be repaired. Did she leave it at the typewriter repair shop? Miss Marple took advantage of this to base her crazy talk on it, perhaps because of that clock, she chose Miss Hyde to be the one who discovered the body\u2014?\" [3168] Hercule suddenly exclaimed loudly:\n[3169] \"You still say this woman is boring and lacks imagination? When did she start planning all this?\" [3170] \"But these were not her plans. That's what makes it interesting. Everything was here, waiting for her. From the very beginning, I discovered this modus operandi, which I was familiar with because I had been reading about it. Very lucky. As Colin will tell you, this week I attended an auction of author's manuscripts. There were some works by Gary Greggson, and I didn't hold out much hope. But luck was on my side. Here\u2014\" Suddenly, he pulled out two old notebooks from the drawer of the table. \"\u2014it's all here! All the plots he planned to write in these books. He died before he could finish them. But Miss Marple, as his secretary, knew all the plots. She could use them to serve her purposes if she wanted to.\" [3171] \"But these clocks must have meant something \u2013 in Mr. Greggson's story, I think.\" [3172] \"Yes, that's right. His clocks were set at five minutes and four seconds, five minutes and seven seconds, and five minutes and five seconds. Together, they form a safe's password, 514547. The safe was hidden behind a replica of the Mona Lisa painting. Inside the safe, \"Poirot continued grumpily, \"was the Russian royal crown. And that's how everything was explained! It was Miss Marple's plot. An innocent girl being framed. Oh, yes, it was easy for Miss Marple. She just had to choose some local characters and make them act according to the plot. So these clear clues led you nowhere! Yes, she is a clever woman. I wonder if Mr. Greggson left her any inheritance? How did he die and what caused his death? I'm curious.\" [3173] Hercule didn't want to hear any more about the past. He put away the notebook and took the hotel business card from my hand. Staring at it, I realized how foolish I had been. [3174] \"Thank you, Poirot, sir,\" Hercule said. \"Your words have given us a lot of inspiration.\" [3175] \"The End\"\n[3176] \"If I have been of any help, I would be very happy.\" [3177] Polo appeared humble. [3178] \"I need to check all the facts -\"\n[3179] \"Of course, of course -\"\n[3180] After bidding farewell, Hercast left. [3181] Poirot turned to me. He frowned. [3182] \"Cheer up. I want to ask you, what's wrong? You look so gloomy.\" [3183] \"I realized how stupid I've been.\" [3184] \"Ah, that's alright. Many people are like that.\" [3185] \"But not you, Hercule Poirot!\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 2:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nAs you know, she teaches at a special school for disabled children.\" [728] \"According to Miss Pemmerton's account, she went out again around 1:30. Do you agree with that?\" [729] \"Well, I can't tell you the exact time, but yes, I do remember her walking past my house.\" [730] \"What do you mean, Miss Watkins? You mean 'she walked past your house'?\" [731] \"Yes, that's right. I was sitting in the living room. The living room faces the street, while the dining room doesn't. We're sitting in the dining room now, and all we can see is the back garden. After lunch, I was sitting in the living room with a cup of coffee, in a chair near the window. I was reading The Times, and just as I was about to turn the page, I saw Miss Pemshaw walk past the front door. Is there something special about that, Inspector?\" [732] \"Nothing special, nothing. I know Miss Pemshaw was going shopping and to the post office. I think the most direct route to the shops and post office would be along the New Moon Street, wouldn't it?\" [733] \"It depends on which shop you're going to, \" Miss Wetherhouse said. \"Of course, the nearest shop is on Albany Road, and there's a post office there\u2014\"\n[734] \"But maybe Miss Pemshaw often passes by your house at that time?\" [735] \"Well, actually, I don't know what time Miss Pemshaw usually goes out or in which direction. I never observe my neighbors' movements, Inspector. I'm quite busy with my own affairs. Some people I know spend their whole day staring out the window, watching who passes by or who visits whom. That's a habit of sickly or idle people. They just love to speculate and gossip about their neighbors.\" [736] Miss Walterhouse's sharp tone made the detective realize she was referring to someone she knew. He quickly said, \"Indeed, indeed.\u201d Then he said, \"Since Miss Pemaroo passed by your front door, she might have been going to make a phone call, right? There's a public telephone booth across the street, isn't there?\" [737] \"Yes, across from number 15.\" [738] \"Miss Walterhouse, there's an important question I have to ask you. Did you see this person arrive here? The mysterious man mentioned in the morning paper.\" [739] Miss Walterhouse shook her head. \"No, I didn't see him or any other visitors.\" [740] \"What did you do between 1:30 and 3:00 pm?\" [741] \"I spent about half an hour doing crossword puzzles in The Times, until I got tired of it, and then I went to the kitchen to wash the dishes I had used for lunch. Let me think. I wrote a few letters, filled out some checks for bills, and then I went upstairs to pick out some clothes I wanted to take to the dry cleaners. I think I heard some commotion next door. I distinctly heard someone screaming, so I instinctively went over to the window. I saw a young man and a girl at the front gate. He seemed to be embracing her.\" [742] Sergeant Lumb shifted his legs, but Miss Wutherspoon did not notice him, and it was clear she did not realize he was the suspicious young man. [743] \"I could only see the back of the young man's head. He seemed to be arguing with the girl. Later, he made her sit down against the doorpost. An odd thing happened then \u2013 he strode into the house.\" [744] \"Did you see Miss Pemmerton come back into the house just before that?\" [745] Miss Wutherspoon shook her head. \"No. I didn't look out the window at first, until I heard the strange scream. However, I didn't pay too much attention to it. Young men often scream, push and shove, laugh silly, or make other noises. These wouldn't make me think anything serious was happening. It wasn't until I heard the sirens of the police cars that I realized something was wrong.\" [746] \"What did you do then?\" [747] \"Well, I casually walked out of the house and stood on the steps, and then went around to the backyard. I wanted to know what was happening, but I couldn't see anything from that position. When I came back to the front, I found that many people had gathered. Someone told me that there was a murder in the house. It was incredible for me. Too incredible! Ms. Walters shook her head repeatedly.\" [748] \"Is there anything else you can think of that you want to tell us?\" [749] \"Actually, I think that's all.\" [750] \"Has anyone recently written to you suggesting you buy insurance, or has anyone visited you or said they were going to visit you?\" [751] \"No, not at all. James and I have both taken out insurance policies with the 'Mutual Insurance Association.' Of course, we usually get some solicitation letters or advertisements, but nothing like what you're talking about.\" [752] \"Have you received any letters signed by Curry?\" [753] \"Curry? No, definitely not.\" [754] \"So, you have no idea who Curry is?\" [755] \"No. Should I?\" [756] Mr. Huckster laughed. \"No, that's not what I meant,\" he said. \"The man who was murdered referred to himself as Curry.\" [757] \"Not his real name, then?\" [758] \"We believe it's not his real name.\" [759] \"Perhaps it's a fake, huh?\" Miss Walterhouse said. [760] \"Unless we find evidence to prove otherwise.\" [761] \"Of course not, of course not. I know you are very responsible.\" Miss Walters said. \"Unlike some people around here who say anything. I'm surprised no one has been sued for defamation.\" [762] \"Defamation.\" Inspector Rune corrected, speaking for the first time. [763] Miss Walters looked at him in surprise, as if she had just noticed his presence. Before, she had thought he was just a subordinate accompanying Inspector Hakaste. [764] \"I'm sorry I couldn't help you. I'm sorry.\" Miss Walters said. [765] \"I'm sorry too.\" Hakaste said. \"With your intelligence, judgment, and observation skills, if you could be a witness for us, it would be very helpful.\" [766] \"I wish I had seen something.\" Miss Watkinson said. [767] At this moment, her tone sounded like a young girl, full of longing. [768] \"Where is your brother, Mr. James Watkinson?\" [769] \"James doesn't know anything,\" Miss Watkinson said with a sneer, \"He'll never know. Anyway, he was at 'Gatesford and Swindham's firm' on Hay Street at the time. Oh, by the way, James can't help you. As I said, he won't be back for lunch.\" [770] \"Where does he usually have lunch?\" [771] \"He usually has a sandwich and coffee at 'The Three Feathers,' a very respectable and cozy place for professionals.\" [772] \"Thank you, Miss Wat Tyler. I'm sorry we kept you for so long.\" [773] He got up and went into the hall. Miss Wat Tyler followed them. Colin Lumb picked up a golf club leaning against the door. [774] \"A good club, this,\" he said. \"Heavy head. I understand you're prepared for anything, Miss Wat Tyler.\" [775] Miss Wat Tyler was a little surprised. [776] \"In fact,\" she said, \"I don't know how this golf club ended up here.\" [777] She quickly took the golf club and put it back in the baseball bag. [778] \"Very clever defense,\" Hakast said. [779] Miss Watford opened the door, and they went out. [780] \"Well,\" Colin Lumb sighed softly, \"we didn't get much from her, despite your usual way of flattering her. Is this your usual method?\" [781] \"It works better with people like her. Flattery is the right approach for such a hard-nosed person.\" [782] \"She purred like a cat who finally got a dozen cheeses,\" Colin said, \"unfortunately, she didn't give us any valuable information.\" [783] \"No?\" Hakast said. [784] Colin quickly looked at him. \"What did you think of?\" [785] \"It's a very subtle and unnoticed detail. Miss Pembroke is going to the post office and the store, but she turned left instead of right, according to Miss Martin's statement, the phone call was made at 1:58.\" [786] Colin looked at him curiously. [787] \"Despite her denial, you still think she might have made that call? She was so certain.\" [788] \"Yes,\" said Hercaster. \"She was very certain.\" [789] He was evasive. [790] \"But if it was her, what was the reason?\" [791] \"Yeah, what was the reason?\" Hakaste said impatiently, \"Why, why? Why so many trivial and unrelated details? If Miss Pemakho made the call, why would she let the girl go? If someone else made the call, why would they frame Miss Pemakho? We're still not clear on anything. If the woman named Martindeil had personally met Miss Pemakho, she would have been able to tell if it was her voice, or at least distinguish if it sounded like Miss Pemakho's voice. Oh, well, we didn't get much information from the 18th. Let's see if the 20th provides some useful information.\" [792] Chapter 8\n[793] The house on Wilbraham Crescent had its own name, Diana Cottage, besides its house number 20. To prevent unwelcome visitors, the gate was locked from the inside with thick wire mesh. The laurel trees looked wilted, with unevenly trimmed branches that made it even harder for anyone to enter. [794] \"If there was ever a house called 'Laurel Cottage', this is definitely it,\" said Colin Lame, muttering. \"I'm surprised they called it Diana Cottage.\" [795] He looked around with a critical eye. Diana Cottage was neither clean nor a garden. The tangled shrubs exuded a pungent odor of cat urine. The house looked shaky, with the gutters long overdue for repair. The only noticeable feature was a newly painted front door. The bright turquoise color stood out against the messy surroundings. There was no doorbell, but there was a door handle, obviously for pulling the door. Detective Hakaste opened the door, and from inside came a piercing sound. [796] \"That sounds like,\" Colin said, \"like 'Marianne's Manor'.\" [1]\n[797] They waited for a moment, and then a strange sound came from inside. A loud chanting, in a form of singing and speaking. [798] \"Damn it -\" Hakaste couldn't help but exclaim. [799] The singer appeared, slowly walking towards the front door, and the singing became clearer to hear. [800] \"No, my little darling. Here, my sweetheart. Sssss - meow. Krrrr - krrrrr. Ah, rrrrrr.\" [801] After a few door slams inside, the front door opened, and a woman appeared before them, wearing a faded green silk tea gown and looking quite old. Her light gray hair was in little bunches, neatly curled, and styled in a fashion that was popular thirty years ago. She wore an orange fur scarf around her neck. Detective Hakaste hesitated before asking:\n[802] \"Are you Mrs. Black?' [803] \"I am Mrs. Black. Be good, 'Sunshine,' be good, naughty boy.\" [804] It was only then that Detective Hakaste noticed the orange fur scarf was actually a real cat. And not just one cat, there were three more in the hallway, two of which were meowing. They lounged comfortably, staring intently at the visitors, tenderly curled up at the hem of the woman's dress. Meanwhile, the smell of cats wafted through the two men's nostrils. [805] \"I am Detective Hakaste.\" [806] \"I suppose you're here because of that repulsive man from the 'Prevention of Cruelty to Animals Association'?\" Mrs. Hem said. \"What a shame! I wrote to him about it. He said my cats were being kept in an environment that was harmful to their health and happiness! What a disgrace! I live for my cats, Detective. They are the only joy in my life. I would do anything for them. Shhh... don't do that, my little darling.\" [807] \"Shhh...\" The cat ignored the hand reaching for it and jumped onto the table in the living room. It sat there, licking its face and staring at the strangers. [808] \"Please come in,\" Mrs. Hem said. \"Oh, not that room. I forgot.\" [809] She pushed open the door to the left. The smell was even more pungent there. [810] \"Come here, my little darling, come here.\" [811] The room was cluttered with various brushes and combs covered in cat hair on the chairs and tables. At least six cats lay on the dirty, faded pads. [812] \"I live for my dear cats,\" said Mrs. Hemm, \"They understand everything I say.\" [813] Detective Hakast strode in. Unfortunately, he was one of those people who was sensitive to cats. As often happens in such situations, all the cats immediately surrounded him. One jumped onto his knee, while another rubbed itself against his pant legs. Detective Hakast, the brave man, clenched his lips and silently endured it. [814] \"May I ask you a few questions, Mrs. Hemm, about \u2013\"\n[815] \"Go ahead and ask,\" interrupted Mrs. Hemm. \"I have nothing to hide. I can show you what my cats eat. They have five sleeping nests in my room, and seven here. They only eat the best fish, all made by my own hands.\" [816] \"This has nothing to do with the cats,\" Detective Hakast raised his voice, \"I came here to talk to you about what happened next door. You must have heard about it, right?\" [817] \"Next door? Do you mean Mr. Joshua's dog?\" [818] \"No,\" Detective Hakast said, \"not that one. I'm talking about a man who was murdered yesterday at number 19.\" [819] \"Really?\" Mrs. Hamm merely said politely, showing no interest. Her gaze remained fixed on her cats. [820] \"Mind if I ask, were you home yesterday afternoon between 1:30 and 3:30?\" [821] \"Well, yes, I was. I usually go out shopping early so I can come back and make lunch for my pets and groom them and dress them up.\" [822] \"Did you notice anything unusual next door? Police cars, ambulances, that sort of thing?\" [823] \"Well, I'm afraid I didn't look out the front window. I went out the back door to the backyard because dear Arabella was missing. She's a little kitten and she climbed up a tree. I was worried she might not be able to come down. I tried to lure her down with a small dish of fish, but she got scared and poor thing. In the end, I had to give up and go back inside. You know, just as I was about to go in, she came down and followed me inside. She looked at Detective, then at Colin, as if to confirm they believed her. [824] \"In fact, I do believe you.\" Colin said, unable to keep silent any longer. [825] \"What?\" Mrs. Hamm looked at him in surprise. [826] \"I like cats,\" Colin said, \"so I've studied their behavior. What you described is exactly how cats behave. Similarly, your cat gathered around my friend, who doesn't really like cats, and they didn't pay any attention to me, even though I tried to coax them.\" [827] Colin spoke almost like a sergeant, but Mrs. Hamm didn't seem to notice, as she merely murmured,\n[828] \"People always know, don't they, sweethearts?\" [829] A beautiful gray Persian cat put its paws on Detective Hakast's knees, looking at him with joy and scratching with its claws, as if Detective Hakast was a pincushion. Unable to bear such teasing any longer, Detective Hakast stood up. [830] \"I'd like to see your backyard, ma'am,\" he said. [831] Colin grinned and smiled slightly. [832] \"Oh, of course, of course. You can do whatever you like.\" Mrs. Hemm stood up and said. [833] The orange cat came down from her neck, and she put the gray Persian cat up there. She walked outside. Poirot and Colin followed. [834] \"We've met before,\" Colin said to the orange cat and then to the other gray Persian cat, \"You're a beauty, aren't you?\" It sat by a Chinese-style table lamp, swishing its tail. [835] \"Please close the door when you leave, sir,\" said Mrs. Hemm in the hallway. \"It's windy today, and I don't want my darlings to get cold. And, there are those naughty boys. It's not safe to let my darlings roam around the garden by themselves.\" [836] She walked to the end of the hall and opened the side door. [837] \"What about those naughty boys?\" Hakast asked. [838] \"Mrs. Lyme's two boys. They live in the south end of the crescent. Our gardens are just a bit behind each other. They're real hoodlums, no doubt about it. They have air rifles, and they've probably had them for a while. I insist that air rifles should be confiscated. They're always sneaking around, and in the summer they shoot apples.\" [839] \"Too mischievous,\" Colin said. [840] The back and front gardens looked equally overgrown, with thick, untrimmed shrubs everywhere and more varieties of laurels than the front garden, but they were tattered and gloomy, with some large fruit trees. In Colin's opinion, Hakast and he were wasting their time. The laurels, other trees, and shrubs formed a dense network, and from here, they couldn't see anything of Miss Pem's garden. 'Diana House' could be considered an independent house. From the perspective of those living inside, there were no neighbors. [841] \"You mean number 19?\" Mrs. Hamm hesitated in the center of the back garden. \"But I think there's only one person living in that house, a blind woman.\" [842] \"The murdered man didn't live in the house,\" the detective said. [843] \"Oh, I see,\" Mrs. Hamm said, looking bewildered. \"He came here to be murdered. How strange.\" [844] \"That,\" Colin thought, \"is a spot-on description.\" [845] ***\n[846] [1] Moated Grange is from Shakespeare's comedy \"Measure for Measure,\" translated by Zhu Shenghao.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 3:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[592] I whistled. [593] \"That's really strange. What's going on?\" [594] \"These damn idiots! I mean, I'm one of them too, I think -\" (Dick is a very honest person) \"You must be very careful, otherwise things will get worse. Well, yesterday the clocks were still properly placed in the living room. I let Miss Pemahyu touch them all to see if she was familiar with them. She said she wasn't. Then they came to move the body.\" [595] \"Then what?\" [596] \"I went out to the front gate to check on other things, then came back inside and spoke to Miss Pemarthy in the kitchen, telling her I had to take these clocks and would give her a receipt.\" [597] \"I remember. I heard you say that.\" [598] \"I then told the lady I would arrange for her to be driven home, and I asked you to take her to the car.\" [599] \"Yes.\" [600] \"Even though Miss Pemarthy said she didn't need a receipt, as the clocks weren't hers, I still gave her one. Then I came to find you. I told Edward to carefully pack the clocks in the living room, and bring them here. All the clocks except the cuckoo clock were packed. Of course, there was also the grandfather clock. That's where I made my mistake. I should have been very specific and told them there were four clocks. Edward said he went in immediately and did what I said. But he insisted that there were only three clocks, besides the two fixed ones.\" [601] \"There wasn't much time between all of this,\" I said, \"which means\u2014\"\n[602] \"It could have been Pemahux. She might have wrapped the clock up and taken it directly to the kitchen after I left the house.\" [603] \"But what about the motive?\" [604] \"We need more information. Was there anyone else? Was it the girl who did it?\" [605] I thought for a moment. \"I don't think so. I\u2014\" I stopped suddenly, remembering something. [606] \"So she might have,\" Hakaste said, \"continue. When was this?\" [607] \"Just as we came out, walking towards the police car,\" I said glumly, \"she forgot her gloves. I said 'I'll go get them for you,' but she said 'Oh, I just know where they are. I don't mind going back to the house now, since the body's been moved.' And then she ran back to the house. But she only left for a minute\u2014\"\n[608] \"Did she have her gloves on when she came back to you, or was she holding them?\" [609] I hesitated. \"Yes, yes, I think she was holding them.\" [610] \"Obviously she wasn't,\" Hakaste said, \"otherwise you wouldn't have hesitated.\" [611] \"She probably stuffed them into her bag.\" [612] \"The problem is,\" Hakast accused, \"you're in love with that girl.\" [613] \"I'm not that stupid,\" I argued, defending myself, \"I met her for the first time yesterday afternoon, and this meeting was far from being the romantic encounter you're implying.\" [614] \"I'm not so sure about that,\" Hakaste said, \"not every girl bursts into a young man's arms, screaming for help in the manner of the Victorian era, making the man feel like a hero, a brave protector. You shouldn't be defending that girl anymore. You know, she's already deeply involved in this murder case and has her own problems to deal with.\" [615] \"You mean this frail little girl stabbed a man to death, carefully hid the knife so you detectives wouldn't find it, and then deliberately ran out of the house, pretending to scream and bumped into me?\" [616] \"If you were me, you wouldn't be surprised,\" Hakaste said with a frown. [617] \"Don't you know,\" I said angrily, \"that I'm surrounded by beautiful spy women from all over the world? They all have measurements that could make an American private detective forget about the black whiskey in his drawer. I'm immune to all female temptations.\" [618] \"Everyone has their Waterloo,\" Hakaste said. \"It's just a matter of what type. Hillary Weber seems to be your type.\" [619] \"Anyway, I don't understand why you're so sure to pin this on her.\" [620] Hakaste sighed. [621] \"I'm not pinning it on her, but I have to start somewhere. The body was found in Pema's house. It involves her. It was the girl named Weber who found the body. I don't need to tell you that the first person to find a body is usually the last one to see him alive. Unless the truth comes out, those two women are not off the hook.\" [622] \"By the time I entered that house at a quarter to three, the person was already dead for at least half an hour or more. How do you explain that?\" [623] \"Hilary Weber's lunchtime is from 1:30 to 2:30 p.m.\"\n[624] I glared at him. [625] \"How is the investigation on Kirby going?\" [626] Hakaste's answer was unexpectedly cold: \"Nothing!\" [627] \"What do you mean, nothing?\" [628] \"It means he doesn't exist, there's no such person.\" [629] \"What about the Big City Small Town Insurance Company?\" [630] \"They have nothing to say because there's no such thing. The Big City Small Town Insurance Company doesn't exist. There's no Mr. Kirby, no Danver Street, no 7 or any other house number.\" [631] \"Interesting,\" I said. \"So you're saying his business card is fake, the name, address, and insurance company are all fake?\" [632] \"That's what it seems like.\" [633] \"What an ingenious idea, don't you think?\" [634] Hakaste shrugged. [635] \"All of this is conjecture. Maybe he's creating a false trail, maybe it's a way for him to introduce himself to get into the house to pull a scam. He could be a con artist, a skilled trickster, a