a bit of a stretch, Edith,\" said Mr. Walterhouse. [672] \"I should have been thinking about who might come here to try and murder me,\" said Miss Walterhouse, becoming quite agitated. [673] Her brother pondered silently, the idea of someone trying to murder her seemed quite impossible. If he were choosing a victim, he would never choose his sister. If someone did attempt it, the assailant would likely be beaten back by a poker or door latch, covered in blood, and handed over to the police in such a humiliating and unusual circumstance. [674] \"I just mean,\" he said, looking more apologetic, \"yes, there have been some unsavory characters around here.\" [675] \"We know very little about what's been happening,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"Rumors are spreading all over the place. This morning, Mrs. Black said some strange things.\" [676] \"I hope so,\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch. He had no intention of listening to the idle gossip from the maids. His sister never wasted time debunking such sensational stories; she only took a moment of amusement from them. [677] \"Some people say,\" Miss Watrous said, \"that this man was an accountant or a trustee at Allerton College, and he came to Miss Pembroke to inquire about some discrepancies in the accounts.\" [678] \"And Miss Pembroke killed him?\" Mr. Watrous seemed amused. \"A blind woman? Are you sure\u2014\"\n[679] \"She quietly wrapped a wire around his neck and strangled him,\" Miss Watrous said. \"He didn't suspect anything, you know. Who would suspect a blind woman? I don't believe it,\" she continued. \"I'm sure Miss Pembroke is a woman of excellent character. We may disagree on many things, but that doesn't mean I consider her a suspect. I just think her views are narrow and stubborn. There are other issues besides education. All these new grammar schools, they look so peculiar, with classrooms made of glass. You might think they were built for growing cucumbers or tomatoes. I'm convinced they're not suitable for children during the summer months. Mrs. Hazes told me herself that her daughter Susan doesn't like the new classrooms at the school. She said because of these glass windows, you always want to look outside, and you can't concentrate on your studies.\" [680] \"Oh, dear!\" Mr. Watrous said, glancing at his watch again. \"Well, I'm afraid I'll be late. Goodbye, dear. Take care of yourself. Remember to lock the door!\" [681] Miss Watrous sniffed disdainfully as her brother left. She closed the door and started up the stairs. She paused to think, walked over to her golf bag, and took out a nine-iron, placing it in a prominent spot near the front door. \"Well,\" Miss Watrous said, satisfied, \"James was just talking nonsense. But it's always better to be prepared. These days, patients are released from mental institutions too easily, encouraged to lead normal lives, and I think it's dangerous for innocent people.\" [682] As Mrs. Hazard hurried up the stairs, Miss Watrous was in her bedroom. Mrs. Hazard was short and plump, like a rubber ball. She enjoyed every big or small event in life. [683] \"There are some gentlemen who want to see you,\" Mrs. Hazard said urgently. \"But,\" she added, \"they're not real gentlemen. They're policemen.\" [684] She handed her the card. [685] \"Inspector Hatch,\" Miss Watrous said. \"Did you bring them to the living room?\" [686] \"No. I left them in the dining room. I've cleared away the breakfast things, and I thought that would be more suitable for them. I mean, they're just policemen.\" [687] Miss Watkins was not very pleased with this reason, but she said, \"I'll go down.\" [688] \"I think they're here to ask you about Miss Pemberton,\" Mrs. Hazle said. \"To see if you've noticed any funny behavior. They say these fits can come on suddenly, with no warning. But usually you can tell by the way they talk, by the way they look in the eyes. But for a blind woman, that's impossible, isn't it? Er\u2014\" She shook her head. [689] Miss Watkins descended the stairs, with a slight air of joyful curiosity, which was really her usual combative spirit, entering the dining room. [690] \"Is it Detective Chief Inspector Hakast?\" [691] \"Good morning, Miss Walterhouse,\" said Huckleberry, rising. Miss Walterhouse didn't even acknowledge the tall, dark-skinned young man who had come with him. She didn't even notice the whispered \"Constable Lamb\" that followed. [692] \"I hope I'm not disturbing you too early,\" said Huckleberry, \"but I thought you'd know why we're here. You've heard about what happened next door yesterday, haven't you?\" [693] \"A murder in my own backyard is never ignored,\" said Miss Walterhouse. \"I've even turned away a few reporters who came asking questions.\" [694] \"You turned them away?\" [695] \"Of course.\" [696] \"You did the right thing,\" Hakaste said. \"Of course, they like to sneak back in, but I'm sure you can handle it with your abilities.\" [697] For such praise, Miss Houseworth allowed herself a hint of pleasure. [698] \"I hope you don't mind us asking you the same questions,\" Hakaste said. \"But if you saw anything that could be helpful to us, I'm sure we would be grateful for your information. At that time, I think you were in the house?\" [699] \"I don't know when the murder happened,\" Miss Houseworth said. [700] \"We think it was between 1:30 and 2:30 PM.\" [701] \"I was in the house, yes, indeed.\" [702] \"Where was your brother?\" [703] \"He doesn't come for lunch. Who was murdered? The local papers haven't mentioned anything.\" [704] \"A stranger?\" [705] \"It seems so now.\" [706] \"You're not telling me that for Miss Pemerton he's a stranger too?\" [707] \"Miss Pemerton told us very confidently that she had no appointment with this particular guest, and she didn't know who he was.\" [708] \"She couldn't be so sure,\" said Miss Watrous. \"She can't see.\" [709] \"We described the person's appearance to her in great detail.\" [710] \"What did he look like?\" [711] Mr. Huckleberry took a photograph from the envelope and handed it to her. [712] \"That's the man,\" he said. \"Do you recognize him?\" [713] Miss Warton looked at the photo. \"No, no... I'm sure I've never seen him before. Oh dear. He looks like a very respectable person.\" [714] \"He does look respectable,\" said the detective. \"He looks like a lawyer or a businessman.\" [715] \"Yes, that's true. There's nothing about this photo that suggests any misfortune. He looks like he's sleeping.\" [716] Hakast didn't tell her that this photo was chosen from among many others, the one that was least likely to cause discomfort. [717] \"Death can sometimes be a peaceful thing,\" he said. \"I think this particular man never expected what was about to happen next.\" [718] \"What did Miss Pembroke have to say about this?\" Miss Walters asked. [719] \"She was very surprised.\" [720] \"That's strange!\" Miss Walters commented. [721] \"Now, please help us out, Miss Walterhouse. If you can remember yesterday, try to think if you saw anything outside the window, or if you happened to be in your garden between twelve-thirty and three o'clock, tell us what happened during that time?\" [722] Miss Walterhouse recalled. [723] \"Yes, I was in the garden... let me think. It should have been before one o'clock. I went into the house from the garden at a quarter to one, washed my hands, and then sat down to lunch.\" [724] \"Did you see Miss Pemahku enter or leave the house?\" [725] \"I think she came back. I heard the creak of the gate. Yes, usually after twelve-thirty.\" [726] \"Did you speak to her?\" [727] \"Oh, no. It was just the sound of the gate that made me look up. That's when she usually comes home. I think she finished her class.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 4:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nI must attack you. Can you tell me, Poirot, is it true that you can see everything from your chair in London, and that you could have let Dick Hercule come to your house to see you, why? Why, I ask you, did you come here?\" [3186]\u201cI told you, they're renovating my apartment.\u201d\n[3187]\u201cThey could have put you in another apartment. Or you could have gone to the Ritz, where you would have been much more comfortable than at the Moulin de la Mouche.\u201d\n[3188]\u201cWell, of course,\u201d said Hercule Poirot, \u201ccoffee, my dear friend, because of the coffee!\u201d\n[3189]\u201cCome on, tell me why?\u201d\n[3190] Hercule Poirot suddenly became angry. [3191] \"Well, because you're so stupid that you can't even guess it, so let me tell you. I'm a human, right? If necessary, I can become a machine. I can think while leaning on a chair. So I can solve problems. But I tell you, I'm a human, and these problems are all about humans.\" [3192] \"So what?\" [3193] \"The reason is as simple as the murder case. It's out of human curiosity.\" Hercule Poirot said, trying to maintain his dignity. [3194] * * *\n[3195] [1] Lady Macbeth is a character in Shakespeare's tragedy \"Macbeth,\" a cruel and evil woman. [3196] Chapter 29\n[3197] I walked again on Wilbraham Crescent, heading west. [3198] I stopped at the door of number 19. This time, no one rushed out of the house screaming. [3199] I walked up to the door and pressed the doorbell. [3200] Miss Mina Pemuk opened the door. [3201] \"I'm Colin Lamb,\" I said. \"Can I come in and talk to you?\" [3202] \"Of course.\" [3203] She led the way into the living room. [3204] \"You seem to have been here for a while, Mr. Lamb. I know you're not a local policeman \u2014\"\n[3205] \"You're right. In fact, I believe you've known exactly who I am since the first day we spoke.\" [3206] \"I don't understand what you mean.\" [3207] \"I'm so stupid, Miss Pemukho. I came here to find you. From the first day I got here, I recognized you, but I didn't know the person I was looking for was you.\" [3208] \"Maybe the murder case distracted you.\" [3209] \"As you say. I'm so stupid that I even misread a piece of paper.\" [3210] \"So, what is it that you're saying to me?\" [3211] \"The game is over, Miss Pemukho. I found the headquarters of the whole plot. You carefully saved these records in the system using braille. The information that Mr. Rakin obtained in Portbury was passed on to you. They were smoothly transmitted to the destination through Lymes. He would come to your house through the garden at night if necessary. One day, while on his way to your house, he dropped a Czech coin in the garden\u2014\"\n[3212] \"He was too careless.\" [3213] \"We all have our careless moments. You did a good job of disguising yourself. You are blind and work in a school for disabled children, so it's natural to have books on teaching blind children at home. You are an extremely intelligent and brave woman. I don't know what power drives you to do this\u2014\"\n[3214] \"I am willing to sacrifice myself.\" [3215] \"Yes, I think so.\" [3216] \"Why are you telling me this? It seems a bit unusual.\" [3217] I looked at my watch. [3218] \"You have two hours, Miss Pemakh. In two hours, specialized personnel from the relevant departments will come to execute the task -\"\n[3219] \"I don't understand what you're saying. Why did you come here before them, giving me these so-called reminders and warnings -\"\n[3220] \"This is a warning. I chose to come here first myself, and I will stay here until our people arrive, ensuring that nothing disappears from here except for one exception. That's you. If you choose to leave, you still have two hours.\" [3221] \"But why? Why?\" [3222] I slowly said:\n[3223] \"Because I still have a glimmer of hope that you will become my mother-in-law... I may be wrong.\" [3224] There was a silence. Miss Pemaroo got up and walked to the window. I didn't take my eyes off her. I had no illusions about Miss Pemaroo. I didn't trust her at all. She was blind, but if you weren't careful, even a blind woman could control you. Her blindness couldn't stop her from taking every opportunity to kill me. [3225] She spoke calmly:\n[3226] \"I won't tell you if you're right or wrong. What makes you think that?\" [3227] \"Eyes.\" [3228] \"But our personalities are nothing alike.\" [3229] \"No, they're not.\" [3230] She almost challenged me. [3231] \"I did everything I could for her.\" [3232] \"It depends on how others see it. For you, career comes first.\" [3233] \"It should be.\" [3234] \"I disagree.\" [3235] Another silence followed. Then I said, \"Do you know who she is? Since that day?\" [3236] \"I found out her name when I heard it... I didn't let her know I existed, not until then.\" [3237] \"You seem to have never been so cruel.\" [3238] \"Don't waste your breath.\" [3239] I glanced at the clock again. [3240] \"Time is ticking away,\" I said. [3241] She moves away from the window and walks towards the table. [3242] \"I have a picture of her here, from when she was young...\"\n[3243] As she pulls out the drawer, I stand behind her. Not a gun. A deadly knife...\n[3244] I grab her hand tightly and take the knife from her. [3245] \"I may be soft-hearted, but I'm not stupid,\" I say. [3246] She reached for a chair and sat down. Despite everything, she remained calm. [3247] \"I didn't intend to take advantage of your kindness. What good would that do? I'll stay here and wait for them. There will always be opportunities, even in prison.\" [3248] \"So, brainwashing?\" [3249] \"If you want to put it that way, go ahead.\" [3250] We sat there, glaring at each other, yet understanding each other. [3251] \"I've quit this job,\" I told her. \"I'm going back to my old field - marine biology. An Australian university has offered me a position related to it.\" [3252] \"I think you're wise. You can't get more from your job. You're like Rosemary's father. He didn't understand Lenin's famous quote: 'Far from sentimentality.'\" [3253] I remembered what Hercule Poirot had said. [3254] \"I'm content,\" I said. \"As a person\u2026\"\n[3255] We sat quietly, each believing the other's point of view was wrong. [3256] A letter from Inspector Hercule Poirot to Hercule Poirot:\n[3257] Dear M. Poirot,\n[3258] We have found evidence that you may be interested in hearing about. About four weeks ago, a Mr. Quentin Dugastlin left Canada for Europe. He has no relatives and the date of his return is uncertain. His passport was found by the owner of a small restaurant in Bremen and turned over to the police. It has not been claimed yet. [3259] Mr. Dugastlin was an old friend of the Montreux family of Quebec. Mr. Henry Montreux, the head of the family, passed away eighteen months ago, leaving a considerable amount of property to his only surviving relative, his niece, Valerie, the wife of Mr. Jocelyn Brand of Portbury, England. A well-known London law firm is handling the Canadian executors. Because the family did not approve of her marriage, Mrs. Brand and the entire family have been cut off from contact with her since her marriage. [3260] Mr. Dugastlin mentioned to a friend that he planned to visit the Brand family when he went to London, as he has always been fond of Valerie. [3261] The body that was thought to be Harry Castleton was actually identified as Quentin Durgeslin. The wooden coffin in which the body was found was later discovered hidden in a corner of the Brand family's backyard. Although paint had been applied to the outside of the coffin to cover it up, experts were able to uncover the words \"Snowflake Laundry\" underneath. [3262] I will not go into further details to avoid causing you unnecessary worry, but the prosecutor believes that an arrest warrant can be issued for Mrs. Brand. [3263] Miss Martinchel and Mrs. Brand, as you suspected, are sisters. Although I agree with your idea that she was involved in the crime, obtaining sufficient evidence is proving to be difficult. She is undoubtedly a very intelligent woman. However, I still hold out hope for Mrs. Brand. She is the type to easily switch allegiances. [3264] Mrs. Brand's first husband died during the war in France, and her second husband, named Hilary Martinchel (who served in the British Army Medical Corps), was also married to her in France. It is clear that they were married there, although detailed records were destroyed at the time. [3265] It was a pleasure meeting you that day, and I must thank you for the very useful advice you provided. I hope your renovation of your London residence is going smoothly. [3266] Your sincere friend,\n[3267] Richard [1] Hakast\n[3268] More information about Hakast's letter to Hercule Poirot:\n[3269] Good news! Mrs. Brand finally confessed! She admitted to everything! She said it was all her sister and her husband who orchestrated it. She \"didn't know about the whole thing until the last minute when they told her what they were going to do, but it was too late!\" She thought they just wanted \"to knock him out so he couldn't tell the difference between the real Mrs. Brand and the fake one!\" This seems plausible! I think she wasn't the mastermind behind it all. [3270] The people at the P\u00f3lo Market confirmed that Miss Martin is the \"American\" woman who bought the two clocks they mentioned. [3271] Mrs. McNeil just said she saw Dugan sitting in Mr. Brand's truck when it went into the garage. Is that true? [3272] My friend, Colin, married the girl. I suppose you'll ask me if he's crazy? Best wishes to you. [3273] Yours,\n[3274] Richard Huckleberry\n[3275] * * *\n[3276][1] Dick is a nickname for Richard.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 5:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[453] \"Alright, alright,\" Dick said. \"You win.\" [454] Chapter 5\n[455] \"Mom!\" O'Neil Courtin stopped and said, sliding a small metal rocket model up and down the windowpane to simulate the rocket's journey through space and into the orbit of Venus, \"Mom, what are you thinking?\" [456] Mrs. Cortin, a stern-faced woman, was busy washing ceramic dishes in the sink and didn't respond. [457] \"Mom, a police car is parked outside our house.\" [458] \"Didn't I tell you not to lie anymore, O'Neil?\" Mrs. Cortin said, roughly placing the dishes on the drying rack. \"Do you remember what I told you before?\" [459] \"I'm not lying,\" O'Neil insisted, \"there really is a police car, and two men got out of it.\" [460] Mrs. Cortin suddenly turned to her child. [461] \"What have you done?\" she demanded. \"Bringing shame and disgrace upon us? That's what you've done!\" [462] \"Of course not,\" Oney said. \"I didn't do anything.\" [463] \"It's those Alf and his gang,\" Mrs. Korting said. \"He and his cronies. A bunch of thugs! I told you, your father told you, they're no good. They always get into trouble. First, they'll be brought before the juvenile court, then sent to a juvenile detention center. I don't want you to turn out like that, do you hear?\" [464] \"They're at the front door,\" Oney said loudly. [465] Mrs. Korting put down her work in the sink and walked over to the window next to her child. [466] \"Hmm,\" she grumbled. [467] Just then, there was a knock at the door. Mrs. Cording quickly wiped her hands with a tea towel and went through the hallway to open the door. She looked at the two men on the doorstep with contempt and suspicion. [468] \"Are you Mrs. Cording?\" the taller man asked politely. [469] \"Yes,\" said Mrs. Cording. [470] \"Can I come in? I'm Detective Hakaste.\" [471] Mrs. Corton reluctantly moved back. She opened the door and gestured for the detective to come in. The room was spotlessly clean, giving the impression that few visitors were welcomed, and this was indeed the case. [472] Enoch, curious, came out of the kitchen and quietly leaned against the door. [473] \"Your son?\" Detective Inspector Hakast asked. [474] \"Yes,\" Mrs. Corton said, defending him with a defiant tone, \"He's a good boy, not what you think.\" [475] \"I believe him to be,\" Detective Inspector Hakast said politely. [476] Mrs. Cottington's hostility subsided. [477] \"I came to ask you a few questions about 19 Wilbraham Crescent. I understand you work there.\" [478] \"I haven't denied that,\" Mrs. Cottington said, still not completely recovered from her previous emotions. [479] \"You work for Miss Millechamp.\" [480] \"Yes, Miss Millechamp is a very kind lady.\" [481] \"Blind,\" said Inspector Hakast. [482] \"Yes, poor thing. But perhaps you didn't know, she has a pair of magic hands, and she can easily cope with life through touch. She can even go out on the street and cross the road by herself. She's different from some people I know who are always whining about nothing.\" [483] \"Do you usually work there in the morning?\" [484] \"Yes. I get there around nine-thirty to ten, and leave at twelve, or whenever I finish my work.\" [485] \"On the contrary,\" thought the inspector, thinking of the four clocks. [486] Mrs. Corten looked at him puzzled. [487] \"What happened?\" she asked. [488] \"A man was found dead in the living room of 19 Wilbraham Crescent this afternoon,\" the detective said. [489] Mrs. Corten's eyes widened. Eunice Corten twisted and turned in excitement, saying \"Oh\" with her mouth open, but then closed it again when she realized someone might see her. [490] \"Dead?\" Mrs. Corten couldn't believe it. And even more puzzling was, \"In the living room?\" [491] \"Yes, he was stabbed,\" he said. [492] \"You mean someone killed him?\" she asked. [493] \"Yes, he was killed,\" he replied. [494] \"Who did it?\" Mrs. Courting pressed. [495] \"That's all we know so far,\" said Huckleberry. \"We thought maybe you could help us.\" [496] \"I had nothing to do with the murder,\" Mrs. Kittredge said firmly. [497] \"Not about the murder, but there are one or two things we'd like to ask you. Did anyone come to see you this morning?\" [498] \"I don't remember anyone. No, not today. Who was he?\" [499] \"He was about sixty, wearing a well-dressed dark suit. He may have claimed to be an insurance agent for a company.\" [500] \"I wouldn't let him in,\" Mrs. Kittredge said. \"There's no insurance agent, and no one selling vacuum cleaners or encyclopedias. Pembroke wouldn't tolerate them, and neither would I.\" [501] \"According to the information on the card he brought, his name is Mr. Corley. Have you ever heard of that name?\" [502] \"Corley? Corley?\" Mrs. Cortlandt shook her head. \"Sounds like an Indian name.\u201d She guessed. [503] \"Oh, no,\" Hercule said, \"he's not Indian.\" [504] \"Who found him, Miss Pemerton?\" [505] \"A young lady, a stenographer, who came here by mistake, thinking she was sent to work for Miss Pemerton. She found the body. Miss Pemerton came home almost at the same time.\" [506] Mrs. Huttington sighed deeply. [507] \"How could this happen,\" she said, \"how could this happen!\" [508] \"We would like to ask you to look at the body of this man, at your convenience, and tell us if you have seen him on Wilbraham Crescent or if he ever visited Miss Pemarhu. Miss Pemarhu is quite certain he never came to her house. There are a few points I would like to ask you about, can you remember how many clocks there were in her sitting room?\" [509] Mrs. Huttington hesitated not at all. [510] \"The big clock was in the corner, they called it a grandfather clock; and there was a cuckoo clock on the wall, with a bird that would pop out and say 'cuckoo.' It didn't startle you, did it?\" She added quickly, \"I never touched any of them, never. Miss Pemarhu liked to wind them herself.\" [511] \"Those two clocks are all right,\" said the detective, reassuring her. \"Are you sure there were only those two in the room this morning?\" [512] \"Of course. Should there have been more?\" [513] \"For example, was there a square silver clock, a traveling clock? Or a gilded little clock on a shelf, or a ceramic clock with a patterned face, or a leather clock with the word 'Rosemary' written on one side?\" [514] \"Certainly not. I have never seen any of those things.\" [515] \"If they were there, you would have noticed them, wouldn't you?\" [516] \"Certainly.\" [517] \"These four clocks are all faster than the cuckoo clock and the grandfather clock by an hour and a half.\" [518] \"Maybe they're foreign clocks,\" Mrs. Cochran said. \"My husband and I went on a trip to Switzerland and Italy, and their time was an hour ahead of ours. It must have something to do with the Common Market. I'm not in favor of the Common Market, and neither is Mr. Cochran. I think British things are good enough for me.\" [519] Detective Hercule Poirot didn't want to discuss politics. [520] \"Can you tell me the exact time you left Miss Pemuk's house this morning?\" [521] \"Twelve fifteen, that's the time,\" Mrs. Kettle said. [522] \"Was Miss Pemuk at home then?\" [523] \"No, she wasn't. She hasn't come back yet. She usually comes back between twelve and twelve thirty, but sometimes she doesn't.\" [524] \"When did she leave home?\" [525] \"Before I got there. I got there at ten o'clock.\" [526] \"Thank you, Mrs. Cottington.\" [527] \"These clocks do look rather strange,\" said Mrs. Cottington. \"Perhaps Miss Pembroke went to an auction market. They're antiques, aren't they? Based on your description, they seem like it.\" [528] \"Does Miss Pembroke often go to auction markets?\" [529] \"Four months ago, she bought a roll of mountain wool carpet at an auction market. The quality was very good. She told me it was very cheap. She also bought some silk curtains. Although they needed to be trimmed a bit, they looked brand new.\" [530] \"But she doesn't usually buy small antiques, paintings, or ceramics like that at auction markets, does she?\" [531] Mrs. Kudin shook her head. [532] \"I haven't seen her do that since I've known her, but of course, it's hard to say about auction markets, isn't it? I mean, you lose your self-control there. When you get home, you'll be thinking, 'What am I doing buying these things?' I've bought six jam jars. I keep thinking I could have gotten them cheaper. The cups and plates are the same. I could have gotten better ones at the Tuesday market.\" [533] She shook her head slightly. Detective Hakaste felt he had no more questions to ask and left. Then Oona began to reflect on the topic they had just discussed. [534] \"Murder! Oh my!\" said Oona. [535] The idea of conquering space was immediately overshadowed by the exciting conversation of the day. [536] \"Miss Pemaroo couldn't have done that, could she?\" he ventured to analyze. [537] \"Don't talk nonsense,\" said his mother. An idea flashed through her mind. \"I don't know whether I should tell him\u2014\"\n[538] \"Tell him what, mom?\" [539] \"It's none of your business,\" said Mrs. Kettlety. \"In fact, there's nothing to tell.\" [540] Chapter Six\n[541] Colin Lumb's Narrative\n[542] 1\n[543] After having two half-cooked steaks and a pint of beer outside, Dick Hakaste satisfiedly sighed and announced that he felt better, then said,\n[544] \"Dead insurance agents, strange clocks, and screaming girls, go to hell! Let's talk about you, Colin. I thought you disappeared from the world. I didn't expect to see you wandering around Croton's back street. I can assure you that marine biologists won't find their calling in Croton.\" [545] \"Do you look down on marine biology, Dick? It's a useful profession. But it's so boring to talk about that people get annoyed when you mention it, so you don't need to explain further.\" [546] \"No chance to show yourself off, eh?\" [547] \"You forget,\" I said coldly, \"I'm an oceanographer. I have a degree in the subject from Cambridge University. Not very good, but at least it's a degree. It's an interesting profession, and someday I'll go back to it.\" [548] \"Of course, I know what you've been doing,\" Hackerst said. \"Congratulations. The trial of Rackin is next month, isn't it?\" [549] \"Yes.\" [550] \"It's amazing how he managed to smuggle the information out. I thought someone would suspect him.\" [551] \"No one would suspect him,\" I said. [552] \"He must be smart,\" Dick commented. [553] I shook my head. [554] \"No, I don't think he's smart. In fact, I think he just did what he was told to do. He could get his hands on very important documents. He took them out with him, had them photographed, gave them back, and then they were sent back to their original location the same day. Everything was arranged in a very tight and compact manner. He developed a habit of having lunch in different places. We thought he always hung his coat in the same place, but it was actually a coat identical to his, and the man wearing it was never the same person. The coat was switched, but the man who switched it never spoke to Rake, and Rake never spoke to him. We wanted to know more technical details. This plan was flawless, and the timing was precise. This person is indeed very smart.\" [555] \"That's why you're still hanging around the Portsmouth Naval Base, right?\" [556] \"Yes, we know the location in the naval base and the location in London. We know when and where Rackin collects his salary and how he collects it. But there's a gap between these two points. There's a subtle and delicate organization between them. That's where we want to know more, because that's where the brainpower comes into play. There's a great command center here, a route that will make you doubtful not once, but seven or eight times. It's a superb plan.\" [557] \"Why did Rackin do all this?\" Hakast asked curiously, \"For political ideals? Promoting individualism? Or for quick money?\" [558] \"He's not an idealist,\" I said. \"I think it's 'just for money'.\" [559] \"If you had started from that angle earlier, wouldn't you have found him faster? He must have spent the money. He doesn't save money.\" [560] \"Oh, that's right! He spent all the money. In fact, I was a bit hasty in dealing with him.\" [561] Hakast nodded understandingly. [562] \"I see. You fell once and then gradually used him. Right?\" [563] \"More or less. Before we found him, he had already taken out some valuable intelligence, so we let him leak more intelligence, obviously very valuable. In my workplace, we sometimes have to act like idiots.\" [564] \"I don't like your job, Colin,\" Hakast said thoughtfully. [565] \"It's not the kind of exciting job people think it is,\" I said. \"In fact, it's usually monotonous, but not just boring. Now people have a feeling that there is nothing that is truly secret. We know their secrets, and they know ours. Our agents are often their agents, and their agents are often ours. In the end, whoever is the double agent, it becomes a nightmare! Sometimes, I think, everyone knows each other's secrets, but they set up conspiracies and pretend not to know.\" [566] \"I see what you mean,\" Dick said kindly. [567] He looked at me curiously. [568] \"I know why you're still in Potterbury. But Croton is ten miles away from Potterbury.\" [569] \"Actually, my real purpose is,\" I said, \"New Moon Street.\" [570] \"New Moon Street?\" Hakast looked puzzled. [571] \"Yes. Or, the moon, the new moon, the rising moon, etc. I started my exploration from Portbury. There's a pub called 'New Moon' there. I spent a lot of time there. It sounds a bit crazy. Then there's 'Moon and Stars', 'Rising Moon', 'Happy Scythe', and 'Cross and Crescent' \u2013 that one's in a place called Simde. Nothing came of it. So I gave up on the moon pubs and started studying crescent-shaped streets. There are a few of them in Portbury. There's Lancelbury Crescent, Addington Crescent, Lifemide Crescent, and Victoria Crescent.\" [572] Suddenly, I saw a confused expression on Dick's face and started laughing. [573] \"Don't look so bewildered, Dick. There's something real waiting for me to start.\" [574] I took out my wallet and pulled out a piece of paper from it, handing it to him. It was a hotel stationery paper, on which a sketch was drawn. [575] \"This is something a boy named Hambley had in his wallet. He put a lot of effort into the case of Racking. He's a good guy, really good. He was hit by a car in London and died. The driver fled. No one saw the license plate. I don't know what this is, but it's something Hambley hastily wrote down or copied, thinking it was important. It made him think of something? Did he see or hear anything? Maybe something related to the moon or the new moon, and the number 61 and the capital letter M. After his death, this note fell into my hands. I don't know what I'm looking for, but I'm sure I'll find something. I don't know what 61 represents, or what M means. I've been busy for three weeks now, within a radius centered on Portbury. I haven't found anything. Cromwell is within my plans. That's it. To be honest, Dick, I don't have much hope for Cromwell. There's only one New Moon Street there, which is Wilbraham New Moon Street. Before asking you if you found any information that can help me, I'm going to walk along Wilbraham New Moon Street first and see what the number 61 looks like. That's what I did this afternoon, but I couldn't find number 61.\" [576] \"I told you, 61 is occupied by a local architect.\" [577] \"That's not what I'm looking for. Are there any foreigners living there?\" [578] \"There might be. Many people do these days. If there are, they'll be listed. I'll check for you tomorrow.\" [579] \"Thank you, Dick.\" [580] \"I'll be doing routine inquiries to the neighbors on either side of 19 tomorrow. Asking if they've seen anyone go to that house, and so on. I'll go to the house behind 19, the one whose garden connects with 19's. I seem to remember that 61 is right behind 19. If you'd like, I can take you along.\" [581] I eagerly agreed. [582] \"I'll be your RAMS officer, in charge of taking notes.\" [583] We agreed to meet at the police station at 9:30 the next morning. [584] 2\n[585] On the second day, I arrived at the appointed time and found my friend seething with anger. [586] After he sent away his sulking subordinate, I cautiously asked him what had happened. [587] For a moment, Hakaste seemed unable to speak. After a while, he blurted out, \"It's those damn clocks again!\" [588] \"Clocks again? What's the matter this time?\" [589] \"One of them is missing.\" [590] \"Missing? Which one?\" [591] \"The leather travel clock. The one with 'Rosemary' written on it.\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 6:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n[847] Chapter Nine\n[848] They drove down Wilbraham Crescent, turned right onto Albany Road, and then turned right again, onto the other side of Wilbraham Crescent. [849] \"That's it,\" Harkness said. [850] \"Once you know,\" Colin said. [851] \"Number 61 is indeed behind Mrs. Hem's house, but it's connected to Number 19 at one corner, which is just great. It will give you a chance to see your Mr. Brand without any outside help.\" [852] \"So that's a beautiful theory,\" the car stopped, and the two men got out. [853] \"Hmm, hmm,\" Colin said, \"look at these gardens!\" [854] This small garden is a perfect example of suburban scenery. There are flower beds with geraniums and African marigolds; lush and plump-leaved begonias; and cute ornaments in the garden - frogs, parasol mushrooms, and funny dwarf statues and fairies. [855] \"I'm sure Mr. Brand is a kind and wealthy man,\" Colin shuddered, \"otherwise he wouldn't have so many unusual ideas. \" When Hakast rang the doorbell, he added, \"Do you think he'll be home at this early hour?\" [856] \"I've called,\" Hakast explained, \"and asked if he's free.\" [857] At this moment, a fashionable small traveling van turned into the garage, obviously a new purchase. Mr. Jotham Brand got out of the car, closed the door, and walked towards them. He was a medium-sized man with a bald head and small blue eyes. He looked friendly and enthusiastic. [858] \"Detective Hakast, please come in,\" he led us into the living room. The furnishings in the room showed the owner's wealth. The room was decorated with expensive and luxurious lamps, a writing desk of the Napoleonic era, a glistening gilded mantelpiece, an inlaid cabinet, and a vase filled with flowers on the windowsill. All the chairs were modern and stylish, with plush cushions. [859] \"Please sit,\" said Mr. Brand warmly, \"smoke? Or do you not smoke when you work?\" [860] \"No, thank you,\" said Hakast. [861] \"Or have a drink?\" Brand said, \"Well, I dare say it's good for both of us. Now, what's the matter? I suppose it's about what happened on number 19? Our garden is connected, but we can't see each other unless we look out the windows upstairs. It seems strange - at least from what I read in the local newspaper this morning. I'm glad to hear from you because it's a good opportunity to get some real news. You can't do anything about all the rumors. It makes my wife anxious, she thinks there's a murderer on the loose. The problem is, how do they let these crazy people out of the mental institution? Is it because they are on parole or for some other reason? Then they go out and do evil to others, and they are sent back to be locked up. As for those rumors! I mean, what our janitor, milkman, and newsboy are gossiping about. You'll be surprised. Some say he was strangled with metal wire, some say he was stabbed, and some say he was beaten to death with a short stick. But in any case, the victim was a man, wasn't it? I mean, not an old woman who was killed? The newspaper reported that it was an unknown man.\" [862] Mr. Brand finally stopped. [863] Hercaster smiled, and said in a nonchalant tone,\n[864] \"Well, about the unknown thing, he left a business card in his pocket with an address.\" [865] \"It seems that the story has added some more information,\" Brand said. \"But you know people. I don't know who made up these things.\" [866] \"Since we're talking about the victim now,\" Hakast said, \"maybe you'd like to see this.\" [867] He took out the police photo again. [868] \"Isn't that him?\" Brand said. \"He looks like an ordinary person, doesn't he? Just like you and me. I don't need to ask if he was killed for a special reason, do I?\" [869] \"It's too early to talk about that,\" Hakast said. \"What I want to know is, Mr. Brand, have you ever seen this man before?\" [870] Brand shook his head. [871] \"I'm sure I haven't. I'm good at remembering faces.\" [872] \"He didn't visit you for any particular reason - selling insurance or vacuum cleaners or washing machines or the like?\" [873] \"No, no. Definitely not.\" [874] \"We should ask your wife,\" Hakast said. \"After all, if he called on you, she would have seen him.\" [875] \"Yes, that's true. I don't know, though... Valerie's not feeling well, you know. I don't want to upset her. I mean, well, this photo of him being murdered, what do you think?\" [876] \"Yes,\" Hakast said. \"That's true. But anyway, it's not a particularly unpleasant picture.\" [877] \"No, not at all. This photo is great. In fact, the guy looks like he's sleeping.\" [878] \"Are you talking about me, Jotham?\" [879] The door to the adjacent room opened, and a middle-aged woman walked in. Hakast thought she had been listening carefully to what was going on in the room next door. [880] \"Oh, there you are, dear,\" Brand said. \"I thought you were napping. This is my wife, Detective Hakast.\" [881] \"That terrible murder,\" Brand's wife murmured. \"I shudder just thinking about it.\" [882] She sighed softly and sat down on the couch. [883] \"Lift your feet, dear,\" Brand said. [884] Brand's wife did as she was told. She was a woman with sandy hair, speaking in a weak voice, pale complexion, and seemed to be enjoying her current state of illness. After a while, she reminded Hakast of someone, but he couldn't remember who. The weak and melancholic voice continued:\n[885] \"My health is not good, Detective Hakast, so my husband naturally lets me avoid things that would frighten or worry me. I'm sensitive. You must be talking about a photo, I think, of the man who was murdered. Oh, dear, that sounds so terrible! I don't know if I can bear to see it!\" [886] \"I really want to see it,\" Hakast thought to himself. [887] He said with a slight malice:\n[888] \"Actually, I think it's best not to show you that photo, Mrs. Brand. I just think you might be able to help us, see if this man ever came to visit you.\" [889] \"I must do my duty, mustn't I?\" Mrs. Brand said, smiling sweetly and bravely. She extended her hand. [890] \"Valerie, have you ever thought this would make you uncomfortable?\" [891] \"Don't be ridiculous, Josea. Of course, I want to see it.\" [892] She looked at the photo with a strong curiosity, and the detective thought the same, but he was disappointed. [893] \"He looks, really, he doesn't look dead at all,\" she said, \"not at all like he was murdered. He was, wasn't he strangled?\" [894] \"He was stabbed to death,\" said the detective. [895] Mrs. Brand closed her eyes and trembled. [896] \"Oh, my goodness,\" she said, \"how terrible.\" [897] \"Have you seen him, Mrs. Brand?\" [898] \"No,\" Mrs. Brand looked reluctant. \"No, I haven't. Is he one of those door-to-door salesmen?\" [899] \"He seems to be an insurance agent,\" the detective said seriously. [900] \"Oh, I see. No, they've never come around here, I'm sure. You don't remember me mentioning anything like that, do you, Josea?\" [901] \"Not at all,\" Mr. Brand said. [902] \"Is there any connection between him and Miss Pembroke?\" Mrs. Brand asked. [903] \"No,\" the detective replied, \"she doesn't know him at all.\" [904] \"Strange,\" Mrs. Brand said. [905] \"Do you know Miss Pembroke?\" [905]\n[906] \"Yes,