f person we want to concern ourselves with. We're looking for someone with a sense of dedication. As for the murder case you're handling, things always seem to go wrong. Now, if Brand had poisoned his wife with arsenic or pushed her into the Aegean Sea, then married a sophisticated blonde \u2013\"\n[933] \"We'll handle it when it happens,\" Detective Huckleberry said. \"For now, we need to continue investigating the murder case.\" [934] Chapter 10\n[935] At 62 Wilbraham Crescent, Mrs. Rymes encouraged herself, \"There are only two days left, only two days.\" She pushed back a few damp strands of hair from her forehead. A tremendous crash came from the kitchen. Mrs. Rymes didn't even want to go over and see what the noise was. If she could just pretend she hadn't heard it. Oh, dear! There are only two days left. She walked through the hall and flung open the kitchen door, the sound much more moderate than three weeks ago,\n[936] \"What have you done?\" [937] \"Sorry, Mom,\" said her son Bill. \"We were using these jars as bowling balls, playing a game, and somehow some of them rolled under the cupboard.\" [938] \"We didn't mean to let them roll under the cupboard,\" said her younger son Ted excitedly. [939] \"Well, pick up these things and put them all back in the cupboard, sweep up the broken ceramics, and throw them in the trash.\" [940] \"Oh, Mom, not now?\" [941] \"Yes, do it now.\" [942] \"Ted will do it,\" said Bill. [943] \"How dare you,\" said Ted, \"always making me do it. If you don't do it, I won't do it either. I bet you'll do it.\" [944] \"I bet I won't do it.\" [945] \"I'll make you do it.\" [946] \"You dare!\" [947] The two children started wrestling fiercely. Ted was pushed hard by Bill, his back hitting the table, and a bowl containing eggs was about to topple over and shake. [948] \"Get out of the kitchen!\" Mrs. Lyman exclaimed. She pushed the children out of the kitchen, closed the door, and began to pick up the jars and sweep up the pieces of broken crockery. [949] \"In two days,\" she thought, \"they'll be back at school! How delightful and happy it will be for a mother.\" [950] She vaguely remembered a witty comment from a column writer:\n[951] On the first and last day of the holidays. How true, Mrs. Rimsay thought, as she swept up the fragments of her beloved dishes. How joyfully she had anticipated their return five weeks ago! But now? \"Tomorrow,\" she repeated, \"Bill and Ted will be back at school. I can hardly believe it. I can't wait!\" [952] How happy they were to see them five weeks ago at the station! Their warm, affectionate greeting! Running around the house and garden. The specially prepared cake for tea. But what did she look forward to now? A quiet day. No more rushing to prepare food, no more constant busyness. She loved these children. They were good children, that was beyond doubt. She was proud of them. But they were also exhausting. Their voracious appetite, their energy, and their noisy voices. [953] Just then, there was a piercing scream. She turned her head alertly. That was it, they had gone to play in the garden. That would be better, there was plenty of space in the garden. But they might annoy the neighbors. She prayed they would stay away from Mrs. Black's cat. Honestly, it wasn't for the cat's sake, but because the wire fence surrounding Mrs. Black's garden was very likely to tear their shorts. She glanced at the first aid kit, which was on the dressing table beside her. [954] This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this silence. She stood indecisively, the basket of broken porcelain still in her hand. The kitchen door opened, and Bill stood there. His eleven-year-old face wore an expression of both fear and ecstasy, unlike anything he usually showed. [955] A sudden, excited scream was cut off, followed by silence. This sudden silence made Mrs. Rimsay feel a sudden panic. Indeed, there was an unusual reason for this si