pregnant, but the man couldn't, or wouldn't, marry her. I never knew which. She was ambitious, and if this got out, she'd have to give up her job. So, naturally, I said I could help her.\" [1313] \"Where is your sister now, Mrs. Rudd?\" [1314] \"I don't know, not at all.\" [1315] \"But she's still alive, isn't she?\" [1316] \"I think so.\" [1317] \"But you haven't kept in touch with her?\" [1318] \"That's what she wanted. She thought a clean break was best for the child and herself, so that's what she did. We both had some extra money left by our mother. Anne gave me half of hers to raise and educate the child. She said she'd continue her teaching career, but at a different school. Later, it seemed she went abroad as an exchange teacher, to Australia or somewhere else. That's all I know, and that's all I can tell you, Inspector Hastings.\" [1319] He looked at her, pondering. Was this all she knew? It was hard to believe these details. Of course, this was all she was willing to tell, but she might not know any more. From these subtle descriptions, Inspector Hastings formed the impression that her sister was a strong, cold woman who would never let one mistake ruin her life. In a state of extreme calm, she had made arrangements for the child's upbringing and happiness. From that moment on, she cut herself off from her misfortune and started a new life. [1320] He thought, he could imagine how she felt about the child. But what about her sister? He said gently:\n[1321] \"It seems strange that she hasn't written to you at all, doesn't she care about the child's growth?\" [1322] Mrs. Ruddle shook her head. [1323] \"If you knew An, you wouldn't think so.\" She said. \"She never hesitated in her actions, and she and I weren't very close. I'm twelve years younger than her. As I said, we've never been particularly close.\" [1324] \"What did your husband think of this adoption?\" [1325] \"I was a widow at the time,\" Mrs. Ruddle said. \"I married young and my husband died on the battlefield. I ran a candy store at the time.\" [1326] \"Where did you live then? Not in Crofton, right?\" [1327] \"No, we lived in Lincolnshire. I used to come here on vacation. I liked it so much that I sold the candy store and moved here. Later, when Hilary grew up and started school, I found a job at 'Roscoe and West', a large clothing retail company. I'm still working there. The people there are very friendly.\" [1328] \"Ah, I see,\" Hercaster said, standing up. \"Thank you very much, Mrs. Luton, for telling me all this honestly.\" [1329] \"But you haven't talked to Hilary yet?\" [1330] \"No need. Unless there's a necessity in the future, if some past events turn out to be related to the murder case at 19 Wilbraham Crescent, I'll come to see her again. As for this, I think it's unlikely.\" He took out the photo he had shown to countless people and showed it to Mrs. Luton. \"Do you recognize this person?\" [1331] \"They've already shown it to me,\" said Mrs. Rotten. [1332] She took the photo and examined it carefully. [1333] \"I don't recognize him. I'm absolutely sure, absolutely sure. I've never seen this person before. I think he doesn't live around here, otherwise I would have remembered. Of course \u2013\" she looked at it carefully. After a moment, she unexpectedly said, \"I think he looks like a good man, a gentleman, don't you think?\" [1334] In the detective's experience, it was an outdated term, but Mrs. Rotten naturally said it. \"People from the countryside,\" he thought, \"they still think that way.\" He looked at the photo again, reflecting, a little surprised that he had never thought of it before. Was the deceased a good man? He had always thought he was a bad man. Maybe it was an unconscious assumption, maybe it was because of some events that had influenced him, that he found a business card in his pocket with a name that turned out to be false. But the explanation he had given to Mrs. Rotten seemed to be true now. Perhaps the name on the card was that of a fake insurance company, and the card was not placed by them. He pondered hard, which made the whole thing even more difficult to solve. He looked at his watch again. [1335] \"I can't keep you from cooking any longer,\" he said, \"but your niece hasn't come back \u2013\"\n[1336] Mrs. Rutt turned and looked at the clock on the mantelpiece. \"Thank God there's only one clock in the house,\" the inspector thought to himself. [1337] \"Yes, she is later than usual,\" she said. \"It's strange. I'm glad Ina didn't wait.\" [1338] Seeing the confusion on Hercule's face, she explained,\n[1339] \"It's a girl from her company. She came here tonight to see Sheila. She waited for a while, and then she said she couldn't wait any longer. She had an appointment with someone. She said she would come back tomorrow or another time.\" [1340] This made the inspector suddenly think of something. The girl he had met on the street! Now he knew why she reminded him of the shoes. Of course. It was the girl who had greeted him at Cavendish Stationery, the one who had been holding a shoe with a broken heel, trying to figure out how to get home. He remembered her as an ordinary girl, not very pretty, with a piece of sugar in her mouth when she spoke. As she passed by him on the street, he hadn't recognized her, but she had recognized him. She seemed hesitant, as if she had something to say to him. He wondered what she wanted to say. Was she going to explain why she had come to see Sheila Webber? Or did she think he was going to talk to her? He asked:\n[1341] \"Is she your niece's good friend?\" [1342] \"Oh, not really,\" said Mrs. Rotten. \"I mean, they're just colleagues, and she's a dull girl, not very lively. She's not particularly close to Hilary. In fact, I wonder why she was so eager to see Hilary tonight. She said something had happened that she couldn't understand, and she wanted to ask Hilary what to do about it.\" [1343] \"Did she tell you what it was?\" [1344] \"No, she said it wasn't important.\" [1345] \"Well, I must be going now.\" [1346] \"It's strange,\" said Mrs. Luton, \"Sheila hasn't called. If it's late, she usually does, because the professor sometimes invites her to dinner. Oh, I hope she'll be back soon.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 8:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \n\"Can we sit down?\" [969] \"Oh, yes, please, sit, sit.\" [970] Mrs. Lymsey sat down on a straight-backed chair, impatiently watching them. She thought this would not take only a little while. [971] \"You don't have to stay here.\" Inspector Hakast said kindly to the two children. [972] \"Oh, we don't want to go.\" Bill said. [973] \"We don't want to go.\" Ted echoed. [974] \"We want to hear you.\" Bill said. [975] \"Yes.\" Ted said. [976] \"Is there a lot of blood there?\" Bill asked. [977] \"Was it a thief?\" Ted said. [978] \"Be quiet, children,\" Mrs. Lyme said. \"Didn't you hear? Mr. Hakaste said he doesn't need you here.\" [979] \"We don't want to leave,\" Bill said. \"We want to hear.\" [980] Hakaste walked towards the door, opened it, and looked at the two children. [981] \"Go out,\" he said. [982] Just two words, spoken calmly and authoritatively, yet a symbol of authority. The two children rose and shuffled out of the room. [983] \"How remarkable,\" Mrs. Rymes thought, with a heartfelt admiration. \"But why can't I do that?\" [984] She pondered on it, for she was their mother. She had heard that these two children behaved completely differently outside than they did at home. Only a mother could make things so difficult. But perhaps, she reflected, it was better this way. If they were friendly, quiet, considerate, and polite at home, and turned into little thugs outside, it would be worse, yes, much worse. When Mr. Hakcastle Detective returned and sat down again, she remembered the purpose of their visit. [9