tching a movie,\" Ted said. [1035] \"Did you leave any fingerprints?\" Bill asked. [1036] \"No useful fingerprints were found. Did you go to the garden yesterday?\" [1037] \"Well, yes, we went in and out all morning,\" Bill said, \"But we didn't hear any noise, and we didn't see anything happen.\" [1038] \"If we had been there in the afternoon, we might have heard the screams,\" Ted said regretfully, \"What a terrible scream it was!\" [1039] \"Do you know Miss Pemshaw, the owner of that house?\" [1040] The two children looked at each other and then nodded. [1041] \"She's blind,\" Ted said. \"But she can walk around the garden freely without any help from a cane or anything. Once she threw a ball back to us, and she threw it straight.\" [1042] \"You didn't see her at all yesterday?\" [1043] The children shook their heads. [1044] \"We don't see her in the morning. She's always out. After tea, she usually comes out to the garden.\" [1045] Colin was examining a hosepipe that ran from the tap in the room to a corner near the pear tree in the garden. [1046] \"I never knew pear trees needed watering,\" he said. [1047] \"Oh, that,\" Bill looked a bit embarrassed. [1048] \"If you didn't,\" Colin said, \"if you climbed up this tree,\" he looked at the two children and suddenly grinned, \"you could squirt the cat with water, right?\" [1049] The two children shuffled their feet on the ground, looking around, not looking at Colin. [1050] \"You did that, didn't you?\" Colin said. [1051] \"Oh, this,\" said Bill, \"won't hurt them. The hose won't, \"he said innocently, \"like a slingshot.\" [1052] \"I suppose you've played with a slingshot before.\" [1053] \"Not really,\" said Ted, \"we've never hit anything with it.\" [1054] \"Well, that hose must have brought you a lot of joy, anyway,\" said Colin, \"and then Mrs. Hamm would come over and complain, right?\" [1055] \"She's always complaining,\" said Bill. [1056] \"Have you ever jumped over her fence?\" [1057] \"There's barbed wire