ple of shrubs here that you might be surprised to see I've kept alive. It's said that shrubs thrive best in Devonshire and Cornwall.\" [1142] \"I'm afraid I'm not much of a gardener,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1143] Mr. McNaughton looked at him as an artist might look at someone who doesn't understand art but knows what they like. [1144] \"Perhaps I should start talking about the unpleasant topic,\" said Mr. Huckleberry. [1145] \"Certainly. It happened yesterday, I was in the garden.\" [1146] \"Really?\" [1147] \"Well, I mean, when I heard that girl's scream, I was here.\" [1148] \"What were you doing?\" [1149] \"Well,\" Mr. MacNoughton said, startled, \"I didn't do anything. In fact, I thought it was those two noisy kids from the Lymeses' house. They're always yelling, screaming, making a racket.\" [1150] \"But the scream came from a different direction, didn't it?\" [1151] \"Yes, if those boys had stayed in their own garden. But they don't, you know. They're always jumping over other people's fences and hedges. They're chasing poor Mrs. Black's cats all over the place. Nobody's watching them, that's the trouble. Their mother's a nice, quiet sort. Of course, when there's no man in the house, the children get out of hand.\" [1152] \"I know Mr. Rimmers often goes abroad.\" [1153] \"He's an engineer, as far as I know,\" Mr. McNaughton said vaguely, \"always on the move. Dam building, or pipelines, or something like that. I'm not criticizing, dear, but I mean this kind of dam building, or laying oil pipelines, or whatever. I don't know the real story. He rushed off to Sweden a month ago, leaving a lot of work for the children's mother\u2014cooking and housework. Well, no wonder they're getting wild. They're not bad at heart, they just need discipline.\" [1154] \"Did you see anything besides hearing the scream? I mean, when did you hear it, by the way?\" [1155] \"I'm not sure. I usually take off my watch when I go into the garden. The other day, the water pipe was broken, and it took me a while to fix it. What time was that, dear? You heard it too, didn't you?\" [1156] \"Probably around half past two, at least half an hour after we had lunch.\" [1157] \"I see. When do you have lunch?\" [1158] \"At half past one,\" Mr. McNaughton said. \"If we're lucky. Our Danish girl doesn't have a good sense of time.\" [1159] \"And then what? Do you take a nap?\" [1160] \"Sometimes. But not today. I need to finish what I started. I cleared up a lot of waste and added it to the compost pile.\" [1161] \"Great, making compost,\" said Hakaste seriously. [1162] Mr. McNuton immediately brightened up. [1163] \"Absolutely right. Nothing beats homemade compost. I've convinced many people of that. Using chemical fertilizers is like self-destruction, isn't it? Let me show you.\" [1164] He enthusiastically pulled Hakaste's arm and pushed the wheelbarrow along the path to the fence separating his garden from No. 19. The compost was surrounded by lilacs, making it look enticing. Mr. McNuton pushed the wheelbarrow to a small shed next to it. The shed was neatly arranged with various tools. [1165] \"You've organized everything so well,\" commented Hakaste. [1166] \"You must take care of your tools,\" McNotton said. [1167] Hakaste closely observed the 19th garden. On the other side of the fence was a path lined with roses, leading to the corner of the house. [1168] \"Did you see anyone in the 19th garden or peering out the window while you were making compost?\" [1169] McNotton shook his head. [1170] \"I didn't see anyone. Sorry I couldn't help you, Detective.\" [1171] \"You know, Angus,\" his wife said, \"I'm sure I saw a shadowy figure hiding in the garden of number 19.\" [1172] \"I don't think you did, dear,\" her husband said firmly. \"I didn't either.\" [1173] \"That woman kept saying she saw this and that, as if she could spot anything,\" Hakaste grumbled as they got back in the car. [1174] \"Do you think she recognized the person in the photo?\" [1175] Hakaste shook his head. \"I doubt it. She's just imagining she's seen him. I know this kind of witness. When I asked her to clarify, she couldn't answer, right?\" [1176] \"That's right.\" [1177] \"Of course, she could have said she saw him on the bus or somewhere else, sitting across from her. But if you ask me, I think it's just her wishful thinking. What do you think?\" [1178] \"I agree.\" [1179] \"We didn't learn anything,\" Hakast sighed. \"Of course, some things seem strange. For example, Mrs. Black, no matter how she tried to associate herself with the cat, shouldn't be completely unaware of her neighbor Miss Pema, which seems impossible. And she was so confused about the murder case, showing no concern.\" [1180] \"She's just a muddled woman.\" [1181] \"No brains!\" Hakaste said. \"When you encounter such a silly woman, fires, thefts, and murders happening around them, they won't even pay attention.\" [1182] \"Her wire mesh fence is quite effective. The Victorian shrubs almost hide everything from view.\" [1183] They returned to the police station. Hakaste grinned at his friend:\n[1184] \"Well, Detective Blue, I can now let you go home.\" [1185] \"No more interviews?\" [1186] \"Not for now. I'll be visiting a few more places later, but not with you.\" [1187] \"All right, then. Thank you for this morning. Will you have my notes typed up? Here's the folder.\" \"You said the interrogation is tomorrow, isn't it? What time?\" [1188] \"Eleven o'clock.\" [1189] \"Good. I'll be back then.\" [1190] \"You're leaving?\" [1191] \"I'm going to London to give a report tomorrow. I can guess who it is.\" [1192] \"Don't say anything.\" [1193] Hakaste grinned. [1194] \"Say hello to that guy for me.\" [1195] \"Also, I might go see an expert,\" said Colin. [1196] \"An expert? What kind of expert? What's going on?\" [1197] \"Nothing, just stupid. Not that kind of expert. He's in your line of work. He's my father's friend, and he's my friend. You know, he loves these kind of things. It excites him. I think he needs a little stimulation.\" [1198] \"Scotland Yard?\" [1199] \"No. A private detective, a friend of my father's, and also a friend of mine. Your bizarre work would be right up his alley. He enjoys these things. It would excite him. I think he needs some stimulation.\" [1200] \"What's his name?\" [1201] \"Hercule Poirot.\" [1202] \"I've heard of him. I thought he was dead.\" [1203] \"He's still alive. But I feel he has nothing to do. It's worse than being dead.\" [1204] \"You're a strange guy, Colin. Having such strange friends. \" \"And you're no different,\" Colin said with a grin. [1205] ***\n[1206] [1] In English, the words \"dam\" (as in a dam) and \"damn\" are pronounced the same. [1207] Chapter 12\n[1208] After sending Colin away, Inspector Hercule looked at the neatly written address on his notepad and nodded. Then he quietly put the notebook back in his pocket and began to deal with the pile of daily files on his desk. [1209] He worked all day. He sent someone out to buy him coffee and sandwiches, received a report from Sergeant Grey - no useful leads were found. No one recognized Mr. Kuri's photo at the train station or bus station. The check of the clothing in the laboratory was also fruitless. The suit was made by a skilled tailor, but the label of the tailor's shop had been cut off. Was the information that Mr. Kuri wanted to hide his identity or the identity of the killer? The detailed report on the deceased's teeth had been distributed to various places, which was likely the most useful clue. It would take some time, but eventually, there would be a result. Unless, Mr. Kuri was a foreigner? Hercule imagined, it was likely that the deceased was French, but his clothes were not French-made, and there were no laundry shop labels to inquire about. [1210] Inspector Hercule was not impatient. Confirming the identity is often a slow process. But in the end, someone would come forward to confirm it, perhaps the laundry shop owner, the dentist, the doctor, or the landlady. Mr. Kuri's photo would be sent to various police stations and would be published in newspapers. Soon, Mr. Kuri's true identity would be discovered. [1211] Meanwhile, there was other work to be done, not just Mr. Kirby's case. Detective Hakast worked non-stop until 5:30 PM. He glanced at his watch again and thought it was a suitable time to visit a few more places. [1212] According to Inspector Gray's report, Ms. Sheila Webber had returned to Cavenish Typewriting Agency and had an appointment with Professor Puddy at the Heron Hotel at 5:00 PM, and she would not leave until after 6:00 PM. [1213] What was the name of the aunt? Mrs. Rotten, Mrs. Rotten. Living at 14 Palmerston Road. He did not drive his police car but chose to walk the shortcut. [1214] It was said that Palmerston Road used to be a lifeless road. Detective Hakast noticed that most of the houses had been converted into apartments or small houses. As he turned the corner, a girl was walking along the sidewalk towards him. She seemed to hesitate for a moment. Detective Hakast was thinking about other things at the time, and he thought she was going to ask him for directions. But if that was the case, why did she change her mind and continue to walk past him? He wondered why he suddenly thought of shoes. Shoes... No, just one shoe. This girl made him feel vaguely familiar. Who was she? Someone he had seen recently... maybe she recognized him and wanted to talk to him? [1215] He stopped and looked back at her. She was walking quickly by now. The problem was, her face had no clear and distinct features, making it hard to remember, unless there was a special reason. She had blue eyes, fair skin, and a slightly open mouth. The mouth. This made him think of something else. What was she doing with her mouth? Speaking? Putting on lipstick? No. He was a bit annoyed with himself. Detective Hakast prided himself on being able to remember people's facial features. He could say that he would never forget the people he had seen in the defendant's and witness's stands, but after all, he didn't just deal with those people. He couldn't remember, for example, every waitress who had served him. He couldn't remember every bus conductor. So he decided not to think about it for now. [1216] He arrives at number 14. The door is half open, and there are four bells beside it with names written underneath. He sees Mrs. Rudd. She lives in the ground-floor apartment. He goes in and rings the doorbell on the left of the hall. After a few minutes, he hears movement. Soon, footsteps are heard from inside. A tall, thin woman opens the door, her black hair disheveled, wearing a large apron, and seems to be out of breath. There is an onion smell coming from the house, obviously from the kitchen. [1217] \"Is this Mrs. Rudd?\" [1218] \"What do you want?\" She looks at him suspiciously and seems a little annoyed. [1219] He thinks she is about forty-five years old and looks a bit like a gypsy. [1220] \"What do you want?\" [1221] \"Could I trouble you for a moment to discuss something?\" [1222] \"Well, what is it? I'm very busy now.\" She spoke quickly, \"Are you a reporter?\" [1223] \"Of course not,\" Detective Hackett said sympathetically, \"I imagine you've been bothered by reporters quite a bit, haven't you?\" [1224] \"Yes, they keep knocking on the door and ringing the bell, asking all sorts of stupid questions.\" [1225] \"That can be quite irritating, I know,\" Detective Hackett said, \"I hope we can help alleviate some of that for you, Mrs. Rutter. I'm Detective Hackett, by the way, in charge of the case that has reporters bothering you. We'll do our best to prevent that from happening, but unfortunately, we can't control the press, you know, they have their own rights.\" [1226] \"It's inconsiderate of them to bother others like that,\" Mrs. Rudd said, \"claiming they have to report the news for the public. I've seen their reports in the newspaper, and they're all full of nonsense. As far as I know, they just make up facts. Please come in, Detective.\" [1227] She stepped back a few steps, and Detective Hakast walked through the doorway. She closed the door. There were several envelopes on the doormat. Mrs. Rudd was about to bend down to pick them up when Detective Hakast politely beat her to it. He glanced at the envelopes, checking the addresses, before handing them over to the hostess. [1228] \"Thank you.\" [1229] She placed them on the table in the hall. [1230] \"Go ahead to the living room, please? I'll be right there. The food in the kitchen is almost ready.\" [1231] She hurried off to the kitchen. Detective Hackett glanced one last time at the letters on the hall table. One was addressed to Mrs. Rotten, and the other two were for Miss R.S. Webber. He followed Mrs. Rotten's directions into the room. It was a small, messy room with old, stained furniture and unidentifiable stains and spots everywhere. There was an eye-catching, expensive Venetian cast glass dish, an abstract model, two colorful velvet cushions, and an exotic-looking large ceramic shallow dish. He thought that either the aunt or the niece had some taste. [1232] Mrs. Rotten returned, panting even more than before.\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask.\n\nPassage 10:\nDialogue between User and Assistant 2026-06-12 03:44:54 \\n<User> The following context is the book I have read: \nThe exact time will be recorded at Somerset House. Where did you get married? Do you remember the location?\" he asked. [2263] \"In a place called Dunbrooke, St. Michael's Church, I think that's the name of the church. I really don't want to think that this happened over twenty years ago. It would make me feel like I have one foot in the grave,\" Mrs. Rival said. [2264] She stood up and extended her hand. Hakaste shook her hand and bid her farewell. He returned to the table, sat down, and tapped the table with his pencil. Soon after, Detective Inspector Gray entered. [2265] \"Satisfied?\" he asked. [2266] \"Seems so, detective,\" said Poirot. \"The name is Harry Castleton, which may be an alias. We'll see what we can find out about this man. It seems that more than one woman is out to get him for revenge.\" [2267] \"He looks quite respectable,\" said Grey. [2268] \"That's just his modus operandi,\" said Poirot. [2269] He began to ponder the clock with the inscription \"Rosemary.\" Was it a souvenir? [2270] Chapter XXII\n[2271] Narrated by Colin Lamb\n[2272] 1\n[2273] \"You're back,\" said Hercule Poirot. [2274] He carefully placed the bookmark in the book. This time, there was a cup of hot coffee on the table next to him. Poirot's taste in beverages was always a mystery! But this time, he didn't offer to share. [2275] \"How are you?\" I asked. [2276] \"Too noisy, too noisy. They're renovating, redecorating, and the whole structure of these apartments seems to have changed.\u201d\n[2277] \"Won't they improve it?\" [2278] \"I suppose so, but for me, it's the most irritating. I have to rearrange my schedule. There's even the smell of paint!\" [2279] He waved his hand, as if trying to brush away these troubles, and asked,\n[2280] \"How's your work going, then?\" [2281] I slowly said, \"I don't know.\" [2282] \"Oh, there may be such a situation.\" [2283] \"I found what they wanted me to find. But I didn't find that man. I don't know what they want. Intelligence? Or a corpse?\" [2284] \"Speaking of corpses, I read the statement from the delayed trial in Crocetta. It said someone was murdered intentionally. The name of the deceased has finally been found out.\" [2285] I nodded. [2286] \"Who is Harry Castleton?\" [2287] \"His wife identified him. Have you been to Crofton?\" [2288] \"Not yet. I'm planning to go tomorrow.\" [2289] \"Oh, do you have any free time?\" [2290] \"No. I'm still busy with work. My work keeps me \u2013\" I paused and continued, \"During my time abroad, I had no idea what was happening. Is it just someone claiming the body? What do you think about this?\" [2291] Poirot shrugged. [2292] \"It's still under investigation.\" [2293] \"Yes, the police have been doing a good job\u2014\"\n[2294] \"Mostly because the wife is very cooperative.\" [2295] \"Mrs. Melina Rival! A nice name!\" [2296] \"This reminds me of something,\" said Poirot. \"What could it be?\" [2297] He looked at me thoughtfully, but I couldn't help him. I knew Poirot, and the name probably reminded him of something. [2298] \"I visited a friend at a country house,\" Poirot mused, then shook his head. \"No, that was a long time ago.\" [2299] \"When I get back to London, I'll come and tell you everything I find out about Mrs. Melina Rival from Hercule,\" I promised him. [2300] Poirot waved his hand and said, \"That won't be necessary.\" [2301] \"You mean you already know everything about her?\" I asked. [2302] \"No, I mean I'm not interested in her\u2014\"\n[2303] \"You're not interested? I don't understand,\" I shook my head. [2304] \"Focus on the essential elements. Now, tell me about the girl named Ina\u2014the one who died in the phone booth on Wilbraham Crescent.\" [2305] \"I've told you everything I know about her. I have no more information on the girl.\" [2306] \"So, everything you know is, or can tell me is, that you saw the girl at the print shop looking like a pitiful little white rabbit, her heel stuck in the grille, and she looked angry\u2014\" He suddenly stopped talking. \"Where was that grille, by the way?\" [2307] \"How would I know, Polo?\" [2308] \"If you asked, you would know. How can you learn details if you don't ask more questions at the right time?\" [2309] \"But knowing where her heel fell off, what difference does that make?\" [2310] \"Perhaps it doesn't matter. In other words, we should know where the girl was at that time, which may be related to the people she saw or the events that happened there.\" [2311] \"You're just making things up. Anyway, I do know it was near the office. She said she bought a round loaf and then limped back to the office in her stockings. She ate the bread there and said she was wondering how she would get home.\" [2312] \"Oh, so how did she get home?\" Poirot asked with interest. [2313] \"I don't know.\" [2314] \"Oh, that's impossible. You'll never get to the bottom of things like this. That's why you only learn irrelevant information.\" [2315] \"You'd better go to Croisset yourself and ask those questions.\" I said angrily. [2316] \"I can't now. Next week there's an auction of a very interesting author's manuscript\u2014\"\n[2317] \"Still your obsession?\" [2318] \"Yes, indeed.\u201d His eyes sparkled. \"Buy John Dickson Carr's works, or as he sometimes called himself, Carter Dickson\u2014\"\n[2319] I left him before he could start, pretending to be rushing to an important appointment. I didn't have the patience to listen to him enumerate the past masters of detective novels. [2320] 2\n[2321] The next evening, I sat on the steps of Harkness's house, and when he came back, I felt as if I had suddenly come out of a depression. [2322] \"Hey, Colin? Is that you? You just popped up again, right?\" [2323] \"It would be more pleasant if you disappeared for good.\" [2324] \"How long have you been here, sitting on my house steps?\" [2325] \"Oh, about half an hour.\" [2326] \"I'm sorry I couldn't let you in.\" [2327] \"I could have easily gone in myself,\" I said angrily, \"but you don't know what training we've had!\" [2328] \"Why didn't you go in then?\" [2329] \"I didn't want to tarnish your reputation,\" I explained, \"if a detective's house was so easily broken into, it would be embarrassing for you.\" [2330] Hakast took out a key from his pocket and opened the door. [2331] \"Come in,\" he said, \"stop making excuses.\" [2332] He walked towards the living room to prepare some drinks. [2333] \"When did you get back?\" [2334] I said not long ago, and we both took our drinks and sat down. [2335] \"We've made some progress,\" Hakaste said, \"we've identified the victim.\" [2336] \"I saw the newspaper report. Who's Harry Castleton?\" [2337] \"A man who seemed respectable. He made a living by marrying or getting engaged to wealthy and gullible women who admired his financial expertise and entrusted their savings to him to manage. Then, he disappeared without a trace.\" [2338] \"He doesn't seem like that kind of person,\" I said, thinking back on it. [2339] \"That's exactly what made him successful.\" [2340] \"But hasn't he been charged with anything?\" [2341] \"No. We conducted an investigation, but it didn't go well. He often changes his name. Although they believe Harry Castleton, Raymond Blair, Lawrence Dalton, and Roger Byron are all the same person, they cannot prove it. Those women, you understand, won't say anything. They'd rather lose their money. This person's name changes constantly, suddenly appearing here or there, always profiting in the same way. It's too unbelievable. There were rumors that Roger Byron disappeared from the south, and a Lawrence Dalton was active near the River Tyne in Newcastle. He's shy about taking photos and always avoids having his girlfriend snap quick shots of him. So, these are events that happened a long time ago, around fifteen to twenty years ago. After that, he seemed to have truly disappeared. There were rumors that he had died, but some said he had gone abroad\u2014\"\n[2342] \"Regardless, there has been no news of him until he died on the carpet in Miss Pemaroo's living room?\" I said. [2343] \"Exactly.\" [2344] \"But there are many possible causes of his death.\" [2345] \"Indeed.\" [2346] \"Women never forget the insults they've suffered,\" I reminded him. [2347] \"You're right. Some women never forget\u2014\"\n[2348] \"If this woman later became blind, wouldn't that be adding insult to injury\u2014\"\n[2349] \"It's just speculation. There's nothing to confirm it.\" [2350] \"What did his wife look like? What was her name? Melina Rival? What an odd name! It couldn't be her name, could it?\" [2351] \"Her real name is Flora Gapp. She came up with it herself. It suited her at the time.\" [2352] \"What was she doing? A prostitute?\" [2353] \"Not exactly.\" [2354] \"What was she, past tense? A socialite, I believe.\" [2355] \"What I mean is that she was a good-natured woman, always there for her friends. She said she used to be an actress. Occasionally did some 'hostess' work. She was well-liked.\" [2356] \"Is all of that true?\" [2357] \"Quite credible. She was very certain when she identified the body. Not a moment's hesitation.\" [2358] \"That's lucky.\" [2359] \"Yes. At first, I was desperate. There were so many letters from wives! Then I began to think that a woman who could recognize her husband was a smart woman. Listen, I think Mrs. Rival knows more about her husband than she's letting on.\" [2360] \"Has she ever been involved in any criminal activities?\" [2361] \"No criminal record. I think she might have had some, maybe still has some, questionable friends. Nothing serious, just messing around, that sort of thing.\" [2362] \"What about the clocks?\" [2363] \"They don't mean anything to her. I think she's telling the truth. We traced the clocks' origins \u2013 the gilded clock and the Dresden porcelain clock were found at the Pombal Market [1]. It was no help at all! You should have seen the place on Saturday. The vendor thought an American girl bought them, but I think it's just a guess. There were so many American tourists at the Pombal Market. Her wife said a man bought them. But she couldn't remember what he looked like. The silver clock was bought from a silversmith in Bournemouth. A tall woman wanted to buy a birthday gift for her little daughter! She only remembers she was wearing a green hat.\" [2364] \"What about the fourth clock? The one that disappeared?\" [2365] \"That's none of your business.\" Huckleberry said. [2366] I knew what he meant. [2367] * * *\n[2368][1] Pimlico Market, located in the Notting Hill district, is one of the most famous outdoor markets in Britain, consisting of three different markets. The antique market at the southern end is the main destination for tourists to shop, and it is only open on Saturdays. [2369] Chapter 23\n[2370] Narrative by Colin Lamb\n[2371] I live in a small hotel near the police station. The barbecue is not bad, but that's about it. Otherwise, of course, it's very cheap. [2372] At ten o'clock in the morning, I called the Cavendish Typewriting Agency to say that I wanted a shorthand typist to type some letters and retype a commercial contract. My name is Douglas Weesby, and I live at the Clarendon Hotel (old hotels always have grand names). Is Miss Hilary Webber available? A friend of mine said she was excellent. [2373] I was lucky. Miss Hilary could come straight over. But she had an appointment at twelve. I said we could finish before then, as I had an appointment at twelve too. [2374] I waited at the Clarendon Hotel's revolving door, and Miss Hilary arrived. I went up to her. [2375] \"Mr. Douglas Weesby is at your service.\" I said. [2376] \"Did you make the call?\" [2377] \"Yes, I did.\" [2378] \"How could you do such a thing?\" She looks a bit angry. [2379] \"Why not? I am prepared to pay Cavendish Typewriting Agency. What difference does it make if we spend our precious time together at the Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 instead of dictating those boring letters? Let's go to a quiet place for a cup of coffee.\" [2380] The Golden Daffodil Caf\u00e9 truly lives up to its name, with its overall decor in bright and bold yellow. The Formica tabletop, plastic cushion, and cups and plates are all in a pale yellow. [2381] I ordered two coffees and scones. It was early, so the coffee shop was only the two of us. [2382] When the waitress took the order and left, we looked at each other across the table. [2383] \"How are you, Hilary?\" [2384] \"What do you mean? Am I okay?\" [2385] Dark circles under her eyes made her eyes appear lavender instead of light blue. \"You're not doing well, are you?\" [2386] \"Yes, no, I don't know. I thought you left.\" [2387] \"Yes. Just got back.\" [2388] \"Why?\" [2389] \"You know why.\" [2390] Her eyes lowered. (Note: This sentence is translated to convey the meaning of the original sentence, but the phrasing could be more natural in English, such as \"She lowered her eyes.\") [2391] \"I'm afraid of him.\" She didn't speak for at least a minute, which felt like an eternity to me. [2392] \"Who are you afraid of?\" [2393] \"Your friend, that detective. He thinks... he thinks I killed that man, thinks I killed Ina...\"\n[2394] \"Oh, that's just him, I reassured her. \"He always looks like he suspects everyone.\" [2395] \"No, Colin, it's not like that. Saying that doesn't help, I won't be happy. He's always believed I had something to do with this case from the start.\" [2396] \"My dear girl, there's no evidence against you. It's just that you happened to be at the scene that day, because someone deliberately made it so you'd be there...\"\n[2397] She interrupted me. [2398] \"He thinks I staged it all, that it's all a made-up story. He thinks Ina must have known something, like Ina recognizing that it was me imitating Miss Pemaroo's voice on the phone.\" [2399] \"Was it your voice?\" [2400] \"No, of course not. I never made that call. I told you that already.\" [2401] \"Look at me, Hillary,\" I said. \"Whatever you say to others, you have to tell me the truth.\" [2402] \"So, you don't believe me either!\" [2403] \"No, I do. You might have made that call for a very innocent reason. Someone told you to do it, maybe told you it was just a joke, and then you got scared, but once you lie, you have to keep lying. Is that how it was?\" [2404] \"No, no, no! How many times do I have to tell you?\" [2405] \"Well, Hillary, you do seem to be hiding something. I hope you can trust me. If Hakaste has anything against you, he hasn't told me anything he knows\u2014\"\n[2406] She interrupted me again. [2407] \"You want him to tell you everything?\" [2408] \"Well, he has no reason not to. We're partners on the same side.\" [2409] Just then, the waiter came over with our order. The coffee tasted as bland as the recently popular beaver fur coats. [2410] \"I didn't know you were involved with the police,\" Hillary said, stirring her coffee slowly. [2411] \"I'm not really a cop. I come from a completely different industry. But I can understand if Dick didn't tell me about you for a particular reason. Because he likes you. Well, I like you. More than like you, maybe. I'm doing this for you, Hill, no matter what you've done. You were terrified when you ran out of the house that day. You were genuinely scared. You weren't faking it. You couldn't pull that off.\" [2412] \"Of course I was scared. I was terrified.\" [2413] \"Was it just seeing the body that scared you, or was there something else?\" [2414] \"What else could there be?\" [2415] I perked up. [2416] \"Why did you steal the clock with the inscription 'Rosemary'?\" [2417] \"What are you talking about? Why would I steal it?\" [2418] \"I'm asking you why you did it.\" [2419] \"I never touched it.\" [2420] \"You said you were going back inside because you forgot your gloves. You didn't wear gloves that day. It was a warm September day. I've never seen you wear gloves. Then, you went back inside and took the clock. Don't lie to me. You did take it, didn't you?\" [2421] She paused for a moment, breaking the scones in the dish. [2422] \"Okay,\" she said softly, almost whispering. \"Okay. I did it. I took the clock and just shoved it into my bag, and then I left.\" [2423] \"But why did you do that?\"\n <Assistant> I have read the book and I will answer the question you ask."